{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779280177428,"sessionID":"ses_1baa43b6fffe0xdByLYL9XEPcW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"tool_write_90OBWJZvp0AdzUesvVDb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r1s0.yaml","content":"id: hyp-20260520-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Precision load-use hazard detection and single 33-bit multiplier sharing\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current 5-stage pipeline has two major microarchitectural bottlenecks:\n  1. Spurious load-use stalls: The hazard unit stalls the pipeline whenever the rd of a load in EX matches the raw rs1/rs2 bits of the instruction in IF/ID. However, many instructions (like LUI, AUIPC, JAL, etc.) do not read rs1/rs2, and many immediate instructions do not read rs2. Treating those raw bits as active register source operands causes frequent, unnecessary 1-cycle pipeline stalls, especially for consecutive loads with immediate offsets.\n  2. Multiplier combinational overhead: The ALU performs three independent 64-bit multiplications combinationally. This consumes a massive number of LUTs and limits Fmax due to the cascading delay in DSP blocks or carry chains.\nhypothesis: |\n  We propose two microarchitectural optimizations:\n  1. Add precise instruction decode information for rs1/rs2 active read signals (if_id_rs1_read and if_id_rs2_read) into the hazard unit. Only trigger load-use stalls if the dependent instruction actually reads the register.\n  2. Replace the three 64-bit multipliers in the ALU with a single shared 33-bit signed multiplier. By dynamically sign-extending or zero-extending the inputs based on the instruction type (signed, unsigned, or signed-unsigned), a single 33-bit signed multiplier can correctly resolve MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU, significantly improving Fmax and reducing LUT4 utilization.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Update load_use_hazard detection to only trigger when the next instruction in IF/ID actually reads the respective source register (rs1/rs2).\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Incorporate instruction opcode decoding to dynamically determine if the IF/ID instruction reads rs1 and/or rs2, and wire these to the hazard unit.\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Refactor the multiplication logic to use a single, shared 33-bit signed multiplier instead of three independent 64-bit multipliers.\"\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r1s0.yaml","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r1s0.yaml","time":{"start":1779280177410,"end":1779280177426}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Formulating a hypothesis**\n\nI'm focusing on creating a hypothesis file, specifically `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r1s0.yaml`. Before proceeding, I'm meticulously verifying the correct path and ensuring adherence to the required YAML schema, which includes an `id` field.\n\n**Refining change paths**\n\nI'm double-checking the file paths within the `changes` section of my hypothesis YAML. It seems I initially overlooked that these need to be relative to `rtl/` or `test/`, not the `cores/<TARGET>/` directory as I might have assumed. I'll ensure the paths adhere to the schema's strict `rtl/...` or `test/...` format.\n\n**Validating file paths**\n\nI'm confirming that file paths within my hypothesis require the `rtl/` prefix, like `rtl/alu.sv`. I've also noted the strict requirement for the exact hypothesis ID `hyp-20260520-001-r1s0` and am checking if the target directory `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/` already exists before writing.\n\n**Constructing hypothesis content**\n\nI'm now populating the `hyp-20260520-001-r1s0.yaml` file. I've included the ID and title, and I'm detailing the motivation behind addressing spurious load-use stalls and the overhead from combinational multipliers. This is shaping up nicely.\n\n**Implementing optimizations**\n\nI am now focused on implementing the proposed optimizations. This involves updating the `hazard_unit.sv` file to precisely check register read signals for load-use hazards and modifying the ALU to use a single shared 33-bit multiplier.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_write_90OBWJZvp0AdzUesvVDb","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e455ce8f6001PYf0pw6grlgGB2","sessionID":"ses_1baa43b6fffe0xdByLYL9XEPcW","messageID":"msg_e455cc462001OotaYMk4X2WQem"}}
