{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779282670005,"sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2979ffeuVyupLTjsOevTe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"tool_read_ud0u9VIbQAqkiuVlcnrs","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl"},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"id\": \"baseline-bench-0ff0685\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-20T12:28:12.197799Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260520-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"(slot 0 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['temp_list_improvements.py', 'temp_parse.py', 'temp_parse2.py', 'temp_read_detail.py']\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current 5-stage pipeline has two major microarchitectural bottlenecks:\\n1. Spurious load-use stalls: The hazard unit stalls the pipeline whenever the rd of a load in EX matches the raw rs1/rs2 bits of the instruction in IF/ID. However, many instructions (like LUI, AUIPC, JAL, etc.) do not read rs1/rs2, and many immediate instructions do not read rs2. Treating those raw bits as active register source operands causes frequent, unnecessary 1-cycle pipeline stalls, especially for consecutive loads with immediate offsets.\\n2. Multiplier combinational overhead: The ALU performs three independent 64-bit multiplications combinationally. This consumes a massive number of LUTs and limits Fmax due to the cascading delay in DSP blocks or carry chains.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"We propose two microarchitectural optimizations:\\n1. Add precise instruction decode information for rs1/rs2 active read signals (if_id_rs1_read and if_id_rs2_read) into the hazard unit. Only trigger load-use stalls if the dependent instruction actually reads the register.\\n2. Replace the three 64-bit multipliers in the ALU with a single shared 33-bit signed multiplier. By dynamically sign-extending or zero-extending the inputs based on the instruction type (signed, unsigned, or signed-unsigned), a single 33-bit signed multiplier can correctly resolve MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU, significantly improving Fmax and reducing LUT4 utilization.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Update load_use_hazard detection to on... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260520-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline 5-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls (~22%), this turns\\nstore bus backpressure into lost execute bandwidth. A timing-local,\\none-deep pending-store slot will decouple execute from these memory stalls,\\nsignificantly improving iterations per cycle (IPC) on store-heavy paths\\nwithout adding Fmax pressure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline 5-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. Under the tournament's random dmem stalls (~22%), this turns\\nstore bus backpressure into lost execute bandwidth. A timing-local,\\none-deep pending-store slot will decouple execute from these memory stalls,\\nsignificantly improving iterations per cycle (IPC) on store-heavy paths\\nwithout adding Fmax pressure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a legal,\\naligned normal-RAM store when io_dmemReady is low. When io_dmemReady is high\\nand the slot is empty, stores drive the existing dmem outputs directly and\\nretire exactly as they do today. When io_dmemReady is low, the MEM stage\\ncaptures the store address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory\\nmetadata into the slot, retires that store once through MEM/WB, and lets\\nyounger non-memory instructions continue while the slot drains. Younger\\nloads, younger stores, and all MMIO/marker/UART stores stall until the\\npending store drains, preserving in-order memory visibility and exact\\ntiming-marker behavior.\\n\", \"expected_im... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260520-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Two-bank registered lookahead instruction replay predictor with static branch/JAL predecode\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline RV32IM core always fetches sequentially and resolves all control flow in EX, incurring a 2-cycle flush penalty on every taken branch and jump. Additionally, the apples-to-apples evaluation environment drives ~22% random instruction-memory stalls, injecting significant bubble counts into the pipeline. Conventional branch predictors or target buffers often regress FPGA Fmax by putting instruction decode, target adders, tag comparisons, or return-address stacks directly on the timing-sensitive fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline RV32IM core always fetches sequentially and resolves all control flow in EX, incurring a 2-cycle flush penalty on every taken branch and jump. Additionally, the apples-to-apples evaluation environment drives ~22% random instruction-memory stalls, injecting significant bubble counts into the pipeline. Conventional branch predictors or target buffers often regress FPGA Fmax by putting instruction decode, target adders, tag comparisons, or return-address stacks directly on the timing-sensitive fetch next-PC path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"We propose a timing-clean, high-performance two-bank registered lookahead instruction replay predictor combined with stateless static branch/JAL predecode in the IF stage:\\n1. Replay Table: A 256-entry (128 per bank), two-bank direct-mapped replay table indexed by pc[8:2] and tagged by pc[31:9]. It caches successfully fetched instruction words and looks up the next-PC replay candidate one cycle ahead, completely removing table reads and tag comparisons from the same-cycle IF decision path.\\n2. iStall Hiding: When imem_ready drops, IF supplies the registered lookahead instruction and asserts fetch-ready, converting random iStall bubbles into zero-wait fetch cycles.\\n3. Static Predecode: Within IF, combina... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(End of file - total 4 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-0ff0685\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-20T12:28:12.197799Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260520-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"(slot 0 hypothesis-gen faile