evant reset structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is built around the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. The strongest recent micro-opt win came from compressing\\nreplay tags and making replay table payload arrays resetless behind valid\\nbits; broader attempts to remove the replay candidate PC guard or apply\\nvalid-only reset discipline across the whole pipeline regressed. That points\\nto a narrower follow-up in the same proven area.\\n\\n`if_stage` still synchronously resets the registered replay candidate PC and\\ninstruction payload to `RESET_PC` and `NOP`, even though those fields are\\nconsumed only when `replay_cand_valid_q` is asserted and the existing full-PC\\nguard matches the current fetch PC. Reset muxes and reset fanout on these\\n64 payload bits sit next to the IF replay/fetch-ready path that now matters\\nmost for Fmax. Clearing only ...\n  Line 50: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-043-r17s0\", \"title\": \"RTL-Only Hot/Cold ALU Opcode Split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM arithmetic from\\nthe universal EX path and gained another large timing win from trimming the\\nregistered instruction replay metadata. The remaining micro-opt opportunity\\nshould therefore avoid new queues, predictors, reset rewrites, or\\nmulti-cycle units, and instead reshape logic that is already on the hot\\none-cycle EX datapath.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU operation encoding and a single broad\\nresult case for the common RV32I operations, the single-cycle multiply\\nresults, and dummy DIV/REM results that are now supplied by `div_unit.sv`.\\nThe two previous hot/cold ALU hypotheses never produced architectural data\\nbecause their implementations were rejected for touching an off-limits test\\nhelper, not because the RTL idea failed formal, cosim, or FPGA placement.\\nThe source still has the same dense opcode/mux shape, so this remains the\\ncleanest unevaluated micro-opt angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM arithmetic from\\nthe universal EX path and gained another large timing win from trimming the\\nregistered instruction replay metadata. The remaining micro-opt opportunity\\nshould therefore avoid new queues, predictors, reset rewrites, or\\nmulti-cycle units, and instead reshape logic that is already on the hot\\none-cycle EX datapath.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU operation encoding and a single broad\\nresult case for the common RV32I operations, the single-cycle multiply\\nresults, and dummy DIV/REM results that are now supplied by `div_unit.sv`.\\nThe two previous hot/cold ALU hypotheses never produced architectural data\\nbecause their implementations were rejected for touching an off-limits test\\nhelper, not because the RTL idea failed formal, cosim, or FPGA placement.\\nThe source still has the same dense opc...\n  Line 53: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-046-r18s0\", \"title\": \"Source-Qualified Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has many pointer\\nupdates, immediate arithmetic, loads, and control instructions after loads,\\nso these false matches can still inject bubbles even after the instruction\\nreplay predictor improves frontend availability.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has...\n  Line 56: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-049-r19s0\", \"title\": \"Bitfield RV32M Control Decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from making the ALU opcode encoding expose a\\nhot RV32I path and a cold RV32M path. That change improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which suggests the next micro-opt should keep\\nexploiting the same encoding rather than adding another replay table,\\npredictor, queue, reset rewrite, or arithmetic unit.\\n\\nSome downstream control logic still decodes RV32M operations with explicit\\nequality trees. In particular, EX detects divide/remainder instructions with\\nfour full `alu_op == ALU_*` compares even though the current encoding makes\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU exactly the `alu_op[4] && alu_op[2]` class. The divider\\nalso reclassifies signed versus unsigned and quotient versus remainder with\\nopcode equality checks even though those are now just low funct3 bits inside\\nthe divide group.\\n\\nThese compares are small, but they sit in always-present EX control and\\nlong-operation stall logic that feeds the frontend hold network. Recent\\nexperiments show broad datapath, reset, and replay changes often lose more\\nplacement quality than they recover, so a narrow decode cleanup in the\\nalready-proven ALU-op encoding is the most attractive micro_opt follow-up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from making the ALU opcode encoding expose a\\nhot RV32I path and a cold RV32M path. That change improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which suggests the next micro-opt should keep\\nexploiting the same encoding rather than adding another replay table,\\npredictor, queue, reset rewrite, or arithmetic unit.\\n\\nSome downstream control logic still decodes RV32M operations with explicit\\nequality trees. In particular, EX detects divide/remainder instructions with\\nfour full `alu_op == ALU_*` compares even though the current encoding makes\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU exactly the `alu_op[4] && alu_op[2]` class. The divider...\n  Line 59: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-052-r20s0\", \"title\": \"Funct3-Aligned Hot ALU Encoding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from an RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split:\\nRV32M operations were moved behind `alu_op[4]`, letting the common RV32I\\nresult path avoid the cold M-extension mux. That improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, so the most promising micro_opt follow-up is to\\nkeep refining the same hot EX datapath rather than adding new replay state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining RV32I/simple ALU encoding is still arbitrary and dense:\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI are assigned sequential localparams, so the\\ndecoder emits constants unrelated to the instruction funct3 bits and the ALU\\nstill needs an 11-way simple-result case. RISC-V already groups the hot\\nOP/OP-IMM operations by funct3, with only SUB/SRA needing a modifier bit.\\nRe-encoding the simple ALU controls around that structure should shrink\\ndecode and mux logic on the single-cycle EX result path while preserving the\\nsuccessful `op[4]` M-extension discriminator.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from an RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split:\\nRV32M operations were moved behind `alu_op[4]`, letting the common RV32I\\nresult path avoid the cold M-extension mux. That improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, so the most promising micro_opt follow-up is to\\nkeep refining the same hot EX datapath rather than adding new replay state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining RV32I/simple ALU encoding is still arbitrary and dense:\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI are assigned sequential localparams, so the\\ndecoder emits constants unrelated to the instruction funct3 bits and the ALU\\nstill needs an 11-way simple-result case. RISC-V already groups the hot\\nOP/OP-IMM operations by funct3, with only SUB/SRA needing a modifier bit.\\nRe-encoding the simple ALU controls around that structure should...\n  Line 62: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-055-r21s0\", \"title\": \"Bank-Aware Replay Fill Select\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses more Fmax\\nthan it recovers, so the next micro-opt should keep the successful two-bank\\norganization and trim redundant logic inside it.\\n\\nThe current banked replay table still does two same-cycle tag comparisons for\\nevery live imem fill:\\n`replay_fill_bank0_match` and `replay_fill_bank1_match` re-read the current\\nPC's indexed set to decide whether to refresh bank 0, refresh bank 1, or\\nreplace the non-MRU bank. But the registered lookahead candidate for the\\ncurrent fetch PC was produced by comparing exactly those two banks on the\\nprevious cycle. If that candidate hits and its PC guard matches the current\\nPC, it already identifies that the word is present and which bank supplied\\nit. If it misses, the previous lookup already proved that neither bank held\\nthe current tag, so replacement can use the non-MRU bank directly.\\n\\nReusing the registered candidate's bank for live fills should remove two\\ncurrent-PC tag compare trees and part of the fill-bank muxing from the IF\\nreplay block while keeping the current replay hit rate, table size, MRU\\npolicy, candidate PC guard, and live-imem-first behavior intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses ...\n  Line 65: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-058-r22s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay MRU State\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for correctness after reset: both replay-bank valid bits are cleared,\\nso the first live fill for a set may choose either bank. After that fill, the\\nMRU bit is immediately written to the bank that was filled and normal\\nreplacement behavior resumes.\\n\\nRemoving reset from this 128-bit replacement-state array is a narrower\\nversion of the reset-elision idea that has already worked for replay\\npayloads. It targets reset fanout and local IF replay placement pressure\\nwithout changing the current-cycle fetch-ready path, replay hit rate after\\nwarmup, branch/JAL prediction policy, ALU datapath, memory behavior, or RVFI\\nretirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for ...\n  Line 68: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-061-r23s0\", \"title\": \"Power-of-Two RAM Range Decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large timing wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split simplified the common\\nresult mux, the RVFI order counter is segmented, and the banked registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall loss. Recent broader\\nmicro-opt attempts that changed replay replacement, reset policy, ALU\\nfunct3 encoding, or source-qualified hazards tended to lose Fmax through\\nplacement noise or extra muxing. The next micro_opt should therefore be a\\nvery narrow equivalence rewrite on logic that is still always present.\\n\\nThe MEM stage still classifies bufferable stores with an unsigned 32-bit\\ncomparison against `32'h0010_0000`. That limit is exactly 1 MiB, so for a\\n32-bit byte address the predicate is equivalent to `addr[31:20] == 0`.\\nThe result feeds store-slot enqueue and MEM backpressure, which in turn\\ndrives the global stall network. Replacing the generic less-than form with\\nan explicit high-bit decode should remove carry/comparator structure from\\nthe store-bufferable path without changing the direct ready-store behavior,\\npending-store semantics, memory ordering, or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large timing wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split simplified the common\\nresult mux, the RVFI order counter is segmented, and the banked registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall loss. Recent broader\\nmicro-opt attempts that changed replay replacement, reset policy, ALU\\nfunct3 encoding, or source-qualified hazards tended to lose Fmax through\\nplacement noise or extra muxing. The next micro_opt should therefore be a\\nvery narrow equivalence rewrite on logic that is still always present.\\n\\nThe MEM stage still classifies bufferable stores with an unsigned ...\n  Line 71: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-064-r24s0\", \"title\": \"Unsigned Multiplier With Signed High Correction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already harvested the broad timing wins: DIV/REM is\\niterative, the ALU has a hot/cold RV32I/RV32M split, RVFI order is segmented,\\nand the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall cycles. The next micro_opt should therefore avoid new predictor state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or latency changes and instead trim logic still\\npresent in the hot EX arithmetic block.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still builds three