n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay tables kept much of the same\\ntiming problem while giving up hit rate.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another direction table\\nor return predictor, but a timing-clean replay predictor: keep the large\\niStall-hiding benefit, while moving the table lookup off the same-cycle IF\\ndecision path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the cycle savings recovered.\\n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay table... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n38: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-031-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Compressed Resetless I-Fetch Replay Tags\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-only cleanups can matter:\\nthe 16-bit segmented RVFI order counter improved Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, while broader datapath rewrites and smaller replay tables\\nlost either hit rate or placement quality. Compressing and de-resetting the\\nexisting replay metadata is the same kind of narrow micro-optimization: keep\\nthe current replay behavior and cycle count, but reduce IF table width,\\nreset fanout, and registered-candidate comparison logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-on... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-032-r13s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Behind I-Fetch Replay\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its large gain from the registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table: random iStall bubbles are often converted into\\nuseful fetches without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF critical\\npath. That leaves the data side as the cleanest remaining structural stall\\nsource. A load still freezes the whole in-order backend whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even when the same normal-RAM word was recently read\\nin CoreMark's compact 6 KB working set.\\n\\nThe earlier standalone MEM-stage load replay experiment was the best\\nstructural signal after the store slot: it reduced CoreMark cycles with a\\nsmall, conservative table located away from the IF predictor path and did\\nnot require load data to feed the EX operand mux. It was not combined with\\nthe current registered instruction replay best. Combining the two targets\\nindependent random-stall sources: IF replay hides instruction stalls, while\\nMEM replay can retire repeated loads during data-memory stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its large gain from the registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table: random iStall bubbles are often converted into\\nuseful fetches without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF critical\\npath. That leaves the data side as the cleanest remaining structural stall\\nsource. A load still freezes the whole in-order backend whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even when the same normal-RAM word was recently read\\nin CoreMark's compact 6 KB working set.\\n\\nThe earlier standalone MEM-stage load replay experiment was the best\\nstructural signal after the store slot: it reduced CoreMark cycles with a\\nsmall, conservative table located away from the IF predictor path and did\\nnot require load data to feed the EX operand mux. It was not combined with\\nthe current registered instruction replay best. Combinin... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n40: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-033-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Two-Word Line I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor result is the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. It kept the large cycle win of the earlier stall-only\\nreplay cache, reducing CoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, while\\nmoving the tag/data lookup off the same-cycle IF decision path enough to\\nreach 472.96 fitness. The predictor experiments after BTFNT and direct JAL\\nshow the useful signal clearly: hiding random imem stalls pays much more\\nthan adding another branch-direction table or return predictor.\\n\\nThe remaining replay loss is likely capacity conflict, not replay timing.\\nThe 64-entry replay attempts gave up many cycles versus the 256-entry\\nversion, so hot text footprint and aliasing matter. Simply doubling a\\nfull-tag word table risks growing the registered lookup mux and tag storage\\nin the IF stage. A better predictor-shaped next step is to pack adjacent\\ninstruction words under one line tag: it increases effective replay\\ncapacity for sequential CoreMark code while amortizing the full PC tag over\\ntwo instructions and preserving the current registered-candidate timing\\nmodel.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor result is the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. It kept the large cycle win of the earlier stall-only\\nreplay cache, reducing CoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, while\\nmoving the tag/data lookup off the same-cycle IF decision path enough to\\nreach 472.96 fitness. The predictor experiments after BTFNT and direct JAL\\nshow the useful signal clearly: hiding random imem stalls pays much more\\nthan adding another branch-direction table or return predictor.\\n\\nThe remaining replay loss is likely capacity conflict, not replay timing.\\nThe 64-entry replay attempts gave up many cycles versus the 256-entry\\nversion, so hot text footprint and aliasing matter. Simply doubling a\\nful... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n41: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-034-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Elide Replay Candidate PC Compare\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result is dominated by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. Compressing the replay table tags improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which shows the replay-hit/fetch-ready path is now\\none of the most timing-sensitive structures left in the design.\\n\\nThe IF stage still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` register and\\ncompares it against the current `pc` every cycle before a replay candidate\\ncan satisfy an external imem stall. That guard is logically redundant with\\nthe current lookahead update discipline: on every clock, the replay candidate\\nis computed for exactly the PC that will be held or loaded into the PC\\nregister on the next cycle. Redirect loads both PC and candidate from the\\nredirect target, a non-stalled fetch loads both from `next_pc`, and a stalled\\nfetch recomputes the candidate for the held PC.\\n\\nRemoving this full-width equality check is a narrow micro-optimization in the\\nsame proven area as the current best. It should reduce IF-side compare\\nfanout, remove 32 candidate-PC flops, and shorten the `fetch_ready` decision\\npath without changing replay hit rate, branch/JAL prediction policy,\\npipeline timing, memory behavior, RVFI reporting, or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is dominated by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. Compressing the replay table tags improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which shows the replay-hit/fetch-ready path is now\\none of the most timing-sensitive structures left in the design.\\n\\nThe IF stage still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` register and\\ncompares it against the current `pc` every cycle before a replay candidate\\ncan satisfy an external imem stall. That guard is logically redundant with\\nthe current lookahead update discipline: on every clock, the replay c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n42: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-035-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Registered MEM Load-Replay Candidate\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small normal-RAM load replay table with a registered candidate for the\\ninstruction entering MEM... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n43: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-036-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Phase-Aligned Replay Candidate\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result comes from the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay predictor. It keeps the large random-iStall\\ncycle win of the 256-entry replay table while avoiding a same-cycle table\\nread in IF. The predictor experiments since then show that adding more\\nbranch-direction or return-target state is not attractive: those changes\\nsaved a few cycles but lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor timing cost is inside the replay fast decision\\nitself. `if_stage` still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` and gates\\nreplay hits with `replay_cand_pc_q == pc` before asserting `fetch_ready`.\\nThat compare is on the current IF stall/PC-enable path even though the\\ncandidate is already generated from the exact same redirect/stall/next-PC\\nselection that updates the PC register. After a clock edge, the registered\\nreplay candidate is phase-aligned with the current PC by construction.\\n\\nRemoving the redundant current-PC equality check is a narrower predictor\\nimprovement than changing table capacity or direction policy. It preserves\\nthe current replay hit rate and CoreMark cycle count while shortening the\\nfetch-ready path and dropping the 32-bit candidate-PC register.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result comes from the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay predictor. It keeps the large random-iStall\\ncycle win of the 256-entry replay table while avoiding a same-cycle table\\nread in IF. The predictor experiments since then show that adding more\\nbranch-direction or return-target state is not attractive: those changes\\nsaved a few cycles but lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor timing cost is inside the replay fast decision\\nitself. `if_stage` still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` and gates\\nreplay hits with `replay_cand_p... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n44: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-037-r15s0\", \"title\": \"Valid-Only Pipeline Payload Reset\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by timing-only wins that do not change\\nCoreMark cycles: the segmented RVFI order counter and the compressed\\nresetless IF replay metadata both improved Fmax by removing unnecessary\\ncarry, compare, and reset pressure from always-present logic. The remaining\\npipeline registers still use broad `<= '0` clears on reset, flush, and\\ninjected bubbles, even though most payload bits are architecturally\\nirrelevant whenever the stage valid bit is zero.\\n\\nThose full-bundle clears add synchronous reset/clear muxes and reset fanout\\nto wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, divide-side metadata, and replay-candidate\\npayload registers. The data fields include PCs, instructions, operands,\\nimmediates, memory words, and RVFI metadata; only a small subset of valid\\nbits and side-effect controls must be known zero to prevent writes,\\nmemory requests, redirects, or incorrect forwarding. A valid-gated payload\\ncleanup is a narrow micro-optimization in the same class as the measured\\nreplay tag reset elision: preserve the exact instruction stream and\\nretirement behavior, but remove needless reset/clear logic from hot\\npipeline boundaries.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by timing-only wins that do not change\\nCoreMark cycles: the segmented RVFI order counter and the compressed\\nresetless IF replay metadata both improved Fmax by removing unnecessary\\ncarry, compare, and reset pressure from always-present logic. The remaining\\npipeline registers still use broad `<= '0` clears on reset, flush, and\\ninjected bubbles, even though most payload bits are architecturally\\nirrelevant whenever the stage valid bit is zero.\\n\\nThose full-bundle clears add synchronous reset/clear muxes and reset fanout\\nto wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, divide-side metadata, and replay-candidate\\npayload registers. The data fields in... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n45: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-038-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Pending Store Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the proven structural pieces: cold DIV/REM is\\nout of the universal EX path, the one-deep pending-store slot hides some\\nnormal-RAM store backpressure, and the registered instruction replay table\\nremoves most random iStall loss. The remaining structural stalls are now\\nconcentrated on the data side. A single pending-store entry lets one stalled\\nRAM store retire, but the next store still freezes the whole pipeline while\\nthe older slot drains, even though both stores already have their address,\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata available in MEM.\\n\\nA two-entry store buffer was attempted before the large post-DIV and replay\\ntiming wins and failed placement on a much larger design. The current RTL is\\nsubstantially smaller and already has the timing-local one-entry direct fast\\npath, so revisiting only a second pending-store slot is the cleanest\\nstructural bet: it targets store bursts under the 22% random dStall model\\nwithout adding load data to EX, changing the branch predictor, widening\\nretirement, or touching the IF replay timing path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the proven structural pieces: cold DIV/REM is\\nout of the universal EX path, the one-deep pending-store slot hides some\\nnormal-RAM store backpressure, and the registered instruction replay table\\nremoves most random iStall loss. The remaining structural stalls are now\\nconcentrated on the data side. A single pending-store entry lets one stalled\\nRAM store retire, but the next store still freezes the whole pipeline while\\nthe older slot drains, even though both stores already have their address,\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata available in MEM.\\n\\nA two-entry store buffer was attempted before the large post-DIV and replay\\ntiming wins and failed placement on a much lar... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(Showing lines 26-45 of 74. Use offset=46 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-019-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Hot-Cold ALU Opcode Split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline control unchanged, but make the opcode encoding expose a\\nsimple hot/cold split that synthesis can map into a shallower mux for the\\ncommon RV32I path. This targets median Fmax with no CoreMark cycle-count\\nrisk and avoids the multiplier-primitive rewrites that previously regressed\\nbadly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline contr... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-020-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Store-Slot-Aware Fetch Skid Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. The better\\nstructural angle now is narrower: use a tiny fetch skid queue only to absorb\\ninstruction-memory backpressure and to keep fetch productive while the new\\nstore slot is draining for older stores. This targets the measured\\nstore-slot cycle win's remaining companion bottleneck without touching the\\nhot EX operand path, load data forwarding, branch predictor state, or RVFI\\nretirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. T... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-021-r9s2\", \"title\": \"X1 Last-Call Return Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already has the predictor features that have proven\\nuseful without excessive timing cost: backward conditional branches are\\npredicted taken and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF. Broader learned\\ndirection tables did reduce CoreMark cycles, but the IF table read and EX\\nupdate routing cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. The return\\npredictor attempts also showed that return bubbles exist, but the 4-entry\\nstack and the PC-tagged one-entry cache added too much frontend comparison,\\nbookkeeping, and routing.\\n\\nThe narrow remaining predictor opportunity is to keep only the useful part\\nof a return-address stack: the standard ABI link register x1. CoreMark is\\ncompiled as ordinary RV32IM C code, so direct calls write x1 and returns are\\ncanonical `jalr x0, 0(x1)`. A single last-call target register can predict\\nthe common innermost return without a BTB, tag compare, multi-entry stack, or\\nconditional-branch table. It intentionally gives up nested-return coverage in\\nexchange for a much smaller IF critical-path footprint.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already has the predictor features that have proven\\nuseful without excessive timing cost: backward conditional branches are\\npredicted taken and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF. Broader learned\\ndirection tables did reduce CoreMark cycles, but the IF table read and EX\\nupdate routing cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. The return\\npredictor attempts also showed that return bubbles exist, but the 4-entry\\nstack and the PC-tagged one-entry cache added too much frontend comparison,\\nbookkeeping, and routing.\\n\\nThe narrow remaining predictor opportunity is to keep only the useful part\\nof a return-address stack: the standard ABI link register x1. CoreMark is\\ncompiled as ordinary RV32IM C code, so direct calls write x1 and r... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-022-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Collapse Redundant EX/MEM Metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycles, bus behavior,\\nbranch prediction, RVFI ordering, or any ISA-visible result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycl... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-023-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-imem path whenever the external\\nbus is ready, and consult a tiny cached copy only on cycles where imem would\\notherwise stall. CoreMark's timed code repeatedly executes the same matrix,\\nlist, state, CRC, and loop-control instructions over a 10-iteration run, so\\neven a small direct-mapped instruction replay table should turn many random\\niStall bubbles into normal fetch cycles after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-im... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-024-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Adder Static Control Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor features are the simple ones that already paid\\nfor themselves: backward conditional branches are predicted taken and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned direction tables and return\\npredictors reduced CoreMark cycles slightly, but each added enough IF-state,\\ncomparison, or EX-to-IF update routing to lose more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to make the profitable\\nstatic predictor cheaper rather than adding more prediction state. Today\\n`if_stage` forms branch and JAL targets with two separate `pc + imm` adders\\nand uses the full adder results for target-alignment gating before choosing\\nthe next PC. Since B- and J-type immediates always have bit 0 clear, the\\ntarget alignment test can be derived from the encoded imm[1] bit and the PC\\nlow bits without waiting for a 32-bit target adder. Branch and JAL are also\\nmutually exclusive opcodes, so one selected-immediate target adder is enough.\\n\\nThis attacks predictor-path Fmax with no intended CoreMark cycle-count risk:\\nthe set of predicted conditional branches and direct JALs remains the same\\nas the current best design for all aligned PCs, and EX still repairs any\\nwrong prediction through the existing resolved-next-PC mismatch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor features are the simple ones that already paid\\nfor themselves: backward conditional branches are predicted taken and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned direction tables and return\\npredictors reduced CoreMark cycles slightly, but each added enough IF-state,\\ncomparison, or EX-to-IF update routing to lose more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to make the profitable\\nstatic predictor cheaper rather than adding more prediction state. Today\\n`if_stage` forms bra... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-025-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-026-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay table should retain\\nmuch of the iStall hit-rate benefit while reducing the IF storage mux,\\ncomparator fanout, and PC-control load that hurt Fmax in the 256-entry\\nversion. This keeps the zero-wait path direct and targets only cycles that\\nwould otherwise be external imem stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay t... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-027-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Small Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should retain much of the random\\niStall hiding while shortening the IF tag/data muxes that hurt the 256-entry\\nattempt. This stays in the predictor slot because the table predicts the\\ninstruction word for the current PC only when the external fetch bus would\\notherwise provide no word; normal ready fetches still use live imem data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should reta... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-028-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Byte-Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the RVFI order counter in `rtl/core.sv` from four 16-bit chunks t... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-029-r12s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\n