 instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use backend-stall cycles only as replay-table\\nwarmup time. The architectural fetch/decode stream remains held exactly as it\\nis today, while a shadow prefetch PC walks ahead through the existing static\\nbranch/direct-JAL prediction policy and fills the replay table with future\\ninstruction words. When the backend releases, later external imem stalls are\\nmore likely to hit in the already-proven registered replay structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-051-r19s2\", \"title\": \"XOR-Indexed I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tables lost hit rate, and adding ways or\\nvictim entries added too much logic. A hashed direct-map index targets the\\nsame conflict misses while keeping the table size, registered lookahead\\ntiming model, live-imem-first policy, and compressed tag storage intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tabl... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-052-r20s0\", \"title\": \"Funct3-Aligned Hot ALU Encoding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from an RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split:\\nRV32M operations were moved behind `alu_op[4]`, letting the common RV32I\\nresult path avoid the cold M-extension mux. That improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, so the most promising micro_opt follow-up is to\\nkeep refining the same hot EX datapath rather than adding new replay state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining RV32I/simple ALU encoding is still arbitrary and dense:\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI are assigned sequential localparams, so the\\ndecoder emits constants unrelated to the instruction funct3 bits and the ALU\\nstill needs an 11-way simple-result case. RISC-V already groups the hot\\nOP/OP-IMM operations by funct3, with only SUB/SRA needing a modifier bit.\\nRe-encoding the simple ALU controls around that structure should shrink\\ndecode and mux logic on the single-cycle EX result path while preserving the\\nsuccessful `op[4]` M-extension discriminator.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from an RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split:\\nRV32M operations were moved behind `alu_op[4]`, letting the common RV32I\\nresult path avoid the cold M-extension mux. That improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, so the most promising micro_opt follow-up is to\\nkeep refining the same hot EX datapath rather than adding new replay state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining RV32I/simple ALU encoding is still arbitrary and dense:\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI are assigned sequential localparams, so the\\ndecoder emits constants unrelated to the instruction funct3 bits and the ALU\\nstill needs an 11-way simple-result case. RISC-V already groups the hot\\nOP/OP-IMM operations by funct3, with only SUB/SRA needing a modifier bit.\\nRe-encoding the simple ALU controls around that structure should... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-053-r20s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Word Store Slot Coalescing\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a pending normal-RAM store is already occupying\\nthe slot and dmem is not ready, a younger store to the same aligned word does\\nnot need a second physical buffer entry. It can be merged into the pending\\nslot because loads and MMIO are still held behind the slot, and there is no\\narchitectural observer between the two normal-RAM stores. This targets the\\nsame store-burst pressure as the failed two-entry FIFO, but with one address\\ncompare and one byte-mask merge instead of another queue entry or a wider\\ndmem arbiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-054-r20s2\", \"title\": \"Banked Registered I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best remaining replay opportunity is to reduce direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses without adding an associative lookup or changing the current-cycle\\nfetch-ready path. A full victim buffer added several tag compares and\\nregressed. XOR indexing changed conflicts but did not improve the measured\\nhit-rate/Fmax product. A banked direct-mapped predictor is a narrower\\nalternative: keep two small direct-mapped replay banks and choose between\\nthem with a simple per-set MRU bit during registered lookahead generation.\\nThe current cycle still consumes only the already registered replay\\ncandidate, so any extra bank selection work stays off the IF replay-hit path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-055-r21s0\", \"title\": \"Bank-Aware Replay Fill Select\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses more Fmax\\nthan it recovers, so the next micro-opt should keep the successful two-bank\\norganization and trim redundant logic inside it.\\n\\nThe current banked replay table still does two same-cycle tag comparisons for\\nevery live imem fill:\\n`replay_fill_bank0_match` and `replay_fill_bank1_match` re-read the current\\nPC's indexed set to decide whether to refresh bank 0, refresh bank 1, or\\nreplace the non-MRU bank. But the registered lookahead candidate for the\\ncurrent fetch PC was produced by comparing exactly those two banks on the\\nprevious cycle. If that candidate hits and its PC guard matches the current\\nPC, it already identifies that the word is present and which bank supplied\\nit. If it misses, the previous lookup already proved that neither bank held\\nthe current tag, so replacement can use the non-MRU bank directly.\\n\\nReusing the registered candidate's bank for live fills should remove two\\ncurrent-PC tag compare trees and part of the fill-bank muxing from the IF\\nreplay block while keeping the current replay hit rate, table size, MRU\\npolicy, candidate PC guard, and live-imem-first behavior intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-056-r21s1\", \"title\": \"Pending Store Slot Load Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currently blocks every younger memory\\noperation until the physical write drains, even if the younger operation is a\\nload of bytes that are already fully present in the pending store entry. That\\ncase does not require a second store-buffer entry or a dmem access: in\\nprogram order the store is already older and retired, so a same-word covered\\nload should read the store slot's bytes. CoreMark's stack, list, and state\\ncode can produce store/load reuse on nearby words, especially under random\\ndmem stalls that keep the slot valid for multiple cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currentl... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-057-r21s2\", \"title\": \"Skewed-Bank I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family structure that has delivered a\\nlarge CoreMark gain and a strong Fmax result: it hides random imem stalls by\\nreplaying hot instructions, while the current IF cycle still chooses only\\nbetween live imem data and an already registered candidate.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are likely conflict misses. The current two-bank\\ndesign stores two tags per raw `pc[8:2]` set, so any hot instructions spaced\\nby 512-byte multiples still contend for the same two physical slots. Earlier\\nalternatives show the tradeoff boundary: a single-bank XOR index changed the\\nconflict pattern but gave up the benefit of two-way capacity, and a victim\\nbuffer added associative compares that cost more Fmax than the extra hits\\nrecovered. A skewed second bank targets the same conflict class with no extra\\ntable entries, no victim search, and no current-cycle table read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family structure that has delivered a\\nlarge CoreMark gain and a strong Fmax result: it hides random imem stalls by\\nreplaying hot instructions, while the current IF cycle still chooses only\\nbetween live imem data and an already registered candidate.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are likely conflict misses. The current two-bank\\ndesign stores two tags per raw `pc[8:2]` set, so any hot instructions spaced\\nby 512-byte multiples still contend for the same two physical slots. Earlier\\nalternatives show the tradeoff boundary: a single-bank XOR index changed the\\nconflict pattern but gave up the benefit of two-way capacity, and a victim\\nbuffer added associative compares that cost more Fmax than the extra hits\\nrecovered. A skewed second bank... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-058-r22s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay MRU State\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for correctness after reset: both replay-bank valid bits are cleared,\\nso the first live fill for a set may choose either bank. After that fill, the\\nMRU bit is immediately written to the bank that was filled and normal\\nreplacement behavior resumes.\\n\\nRemoving reset from this 128-bit replacement-state array is a narrower\\nversion of the reset-elision idea that has already worked for replay\\npayloads. It targets reset fanout and local IF replay placement pressure\\nwithout changing the current-cycle fetch-ready path, replay hit rate after\\nwarmup, branch/JAL prediction policy, ALU datapath, memory behavior, or RVFI\\nretirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)","truncated":true,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl","time":{"start":1779282738505,"end":1779282738509}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_read_wozgUXA2LKCV4AYH0KtI","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e4583fd4700282iU1Gvdyf5NLk","sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2979ffeuVyupLTjsOevTe","messageID":"msg_e4583e2ea001aKpqR5kUa3fffI"}}
