": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage ALU result path as ADD, address generation, branch\\ncompare support, and MUL. The generated FPGA critical paths are dominated by\\nlong EX-stage carry/mux structures, while the CoreMark timed loop is\\neffectively division-cold: the static binary has only a few DIVU sites, and\\nthose are in setup/printing paths rather than the hot matrix/list/state\\niteration body. Prior multiplier and MEM-lane rewrites showed that reshaping\\nalready-mapped hot datapaths can badly hurt placement; the better\\nmicro-optimization is to remove the cold divider/remainder hardware from the\\none-cycle ALU path entirely while leaving multiply and all RV32I operations\\nsingle-cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage ALU result path as ADD, address generation, branch\\ncompare support, and MUL. The generated FPGA critical paths are dominated by\\nlong EX-stage carry/mux structures, while the CoreMark timed loop is\\neffectively division-cold: the static binary has only a few DIVU sites, and\\nthose are in setup/printing paths rather than the hot matrix/list/state\\niteration body. Prior multiplier and MEM-lane rewrites showed that reshaping\\nalready-mapped hot datapaths can badly hurt placement; the better\\nmicro-optimization is to remove the cold divider/remainder hardware from the\\none-cycle ALU path entirely while leaving multiply and all RV32I operations\\nsingle-cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases with a small\\niterative divider/remainder unit that is only entered for those four ALU\\nopcodes. Normal RV32I operations, branches, jumps, loads/stores, and all MUL\\nvariants remain one-cycle and keep the current forwarding behavior. When a\\ndivide-class instruction reaches...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-025-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", ...\n  Line 38: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-031-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Compressed Resetless I-Fetch Replay Tags\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-only cleanups can matter:\\nthe 16-bit segmented RVFI order counter improved Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, while broader datapath rewrites and smaller replay tables\\nlost either hit rate or placement quality. Compressing and de-resetting the\\nexisting replay metadata is the same kind of narrow micro-optimization: keep\\nthe current replay behavior and cycle count, but reduce IF table width,\\nreset fanout, and registered-candidate comparison logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-on...\n  Line 50: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-043-r17s0\", \"title\": \"RTL-Only Hot/Cold ALU Opcode Split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM arithmetic from\\nthe universal EX path and gained another large timing win from trimming the\\nregistered instruction replay metadata. The remaining micro-opt opportunity\\nshould therefore avoid new queues, predictors, reset rewrites, or\\nmulti-cycle units, and instead reshape logic that is already on the hot\\none-cycle EX datapath.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU operation encoding and a single broad\\nresult case for the common RV32I operations, the single-cycle multiply\\nresults, and dummy DIV/REM results that are now supplied by `div_unit.sv`.\\nThe two previous hot/cold ALU hypotheses never produced architectural data\\nbecause their implementations were rejected for touching an off-limits test\\nhelper, not because the RTL idea failed formal, cosim, or FPGA placement.\\nThe source still has the same dense opcode/mux shape, so this remains the\\ncleanest unevaluated micro-opt angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM arithmetic from\\nthe universal EX path and gained another large timing win from trimming the\\nregistered instruction replay metadata. The remaining micro-opt opportunity\\nshould therefore avoid new queues, predictors, reset rewrites, or\\nmulti-cycle units, and instead reshape logic that is already on the hot\\none-cycle EX datapath.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU operation encoding and a single broad\\nresult case for the common RV32I operations, the single-cycle multiply\\nresults, and dummy DIV/REM results that are now supplied by `div_unit.sv`.\\nThe two previous hot/cold ALU hypotheses never produced architectural data\\nbecause their implementations were rejected for touching an off-limits test\\nhelper, not because the RTL idea failed formal, cosim, or FPGA placement.\\nThe source still has the same dense opc...","metadata":{"matches":5,"truncated":false},"title":"\"category\": \"micro_opt\".*\"outcome\": \"improvement\"","time":{"start":1779282751372,"end":1779282751379}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Exploring Hypothesis History**\n\nI'm currently reviewing the extensive hypothesis history available in `cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl`. My focus is on synthesizing past explorations to inform a novel architectural hypothesis for RV3.\n\n**Investigating Micro-Optimizations**\n\nI'm zeroing in on successful micro-optimization hypotheses, specifically analyzing `hyp-20260427-001-r1s0` which moved DIV/REM off the single-cycle ALU. I've confirmed that DIV/REM is still combinational in the `cores/bench/` module, raising questions about its baseline state compared to that prior optimization.\n\n**Revisiting Core Benchmarks**\n\nI'm scrutinizing the `cores/bench/rtl/` for existing predictor implementations and noticed that the `bench` core has already seen micro-optimization attempts. I'm comparing our current generation of `hyp-20260520-001-r2s0` against past improvements in the `v1` log.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_grep_2BOHETvbI0IEqcLwiG37","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e45842f89001w6IYgg9Vi9DKtR","sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2979ffeuVyupLTjsOevTe","messageID":"msg_e4584161d001jthHtJsptteavT"}}
