{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779282977498,"sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2979ffeuVyupLTjsOevTe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"tool_write_C969XhvOJh7DqH9bDsG6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r2s0.yaml","content":"id: hyp-20260520-001-r2s0\ntitle: \"Segmented 16-Bit Carry-Cut RVFI Order Counter\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current 5-stage in-order core uses a single monolithic 64-bit register and a 64-bit adder to track the RVFI instruction retirement order (rvfi_order_q). The FPGA benchmark evaluation harness preserves the RVFI outputs to verify correct operation and prevent optimization dead-ends, which means this 64-bit addition remains physically present on the FPGA. A full 64-bit combinational carry-chain addition competes directly with the core's hot critical execution paths for routing and logic budget, limiting the overall achievable Fmax of the Tang Nano 20K design without providing any architectural benefit to the actual instruction-execution rate.\nhypothesis: |\n  We propose to segment the monolithic 64-bit RVFI retirement order counter into four independent 16-bit carry-cut chunks: a low chunk, a mid-low chunk, a mid-high chunk, and a high chunk. Each chunk only performs a 16-bit addition when the immediately preceding chunk overflows, completely breaking the 64-bit long carry chain into four timing-isolated 16-bit chains. This microarchitectural refinement will reduce critical-path delay, lower routing congestion, and improve placement efficiency during synthesis and place-and-route on the Tang Nano 20K FPGA, resulting in a higher Fmax. Since the overall retirement sequencing behavior is preserved bit-for-bit, this timing-only change maintains perfect compatibility with riscv-formal and the cosimulation testbenches while incurring zero IPC cycles penalty.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Replace the monolithic 64-bit rvfi_order_q counter register with four 16-bit registers (rvfi_order_lo_q, rvfi_order_mid_lo_q, rvfi_order_mid_hi_q, rvfi_order_hi_q) and sequential conditional increment logic that updates them on overflow of the younger chunks.\"\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r2s0.yaml","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r2s0.yaml","time":{"start":1779282977489,"end":1779282977497}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Confirming timing improvements**\n\nI've identified a significant Fmax timing cleanup, achieving a delta percentage within the 6.0% to 9.9% range. Before proceeding, I'm carefully reviewing the schema to ensure all necessary components are accounted for and to prevent any errors in documentation.\n\n**Segmenting counters**\n\nI'm exploring a micro-optimization involving the segmentation of a 64-bit RVFI order counter. My goal is to divide it into four 16-bit carry-cut chunks, aiming for a fitness delta of at least 5%. I'll be implementing this change in `rtl/core.sv`.\n\n**Drafting hypothesis file**\n\nI'm currently focused on structuring the hypothesis YAML for the segmented counter optimization. My immediate task is to meticulously define its `id`, `title`, `category`, and `motivation`, ensuring precise alignment with the required schema. I need to get this right before I can proceed with generating the actual file.\n\n**Optimizing counter design**\n\nI'm refining the RVFI retirement order counter by segmenting the 64-bit addition into four 16-bit carry-cut chunks. This approach aims to break the long carry chain, thereby reducing critical-path delay and improving Fmax, particularly for the Tang Nano 20K. The expected fitness delta is at least 6%, with high confidence in this timing-focused optimization.\n\n**Validating YAML Schema**\n\nI'm currently focused on meticulously validating all fields within the hypothesis YAML against the established schema. My priority is ensuring that `fitness_delta_pct` is indeed an integer as required, before I can formally finalize and generate the document.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_write_C969XhvOJh7DqH9bDsG6","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e4587a2cd001oUNb5z8klLJkeB","sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2979ffeuVyupLTjsOevTe","messageID":"msg_e45877e54001n19IiOUNs75J66"}}
