{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779282713488,"sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2a2bffepcCr3DVoK1N8UH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"tool_bash_fgQbOcjqc6pp0uO7TDCH","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python temp_read_all_structural.py","description":"Runs Python script to print the full motivation and hypothesis for hyp-20260428-011-r6s1 and hyp-20260428-017-r8s1"},"output":"ID: hyp-20260428-011-r6s1\nTitle: Registered Fetch/Decode Cutpoint\nCategory: structural\nOutcome: regression\nFitness: 330.52\nMotivation:\nThe current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\n\nHypothesis:\n{\n  \"motivation\": \"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID handoff into a real registered pipeline boundary with no zero-latency imem-data-to-decode bypass. IF captures {pc, instr, predicted_taken, predicted_target, valid} when io_imemReady is high and the decode side can accept an entry; ID decodes only the registered entry on the following cycle. Redirects and reset flush the IF/ID register and restart fetch at the resolved target, while load-use and dmem stalls hold the registered entry instead of re-decoding live imem data. The existing backward-branch and direct-JAL prediction still choose the fetch PC in IF, so correctly predicted hot loops and direct jumps should keep one instruction per cycle after fill. The expected win is a shorter critical path and better Fmax; the expected cost is only an extra recovery cycle for residual mispredictions and unpredicted JALR returns, which recent results suggest are smaller than the timing headroom available from cutting the frontend path.\\n\",\n  \"expected_impact\": {\n    \"fitness_delta_pct\": 5,\n    \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID output with an internal IF/ID register. Capture fetched instruction metadata only on io_imemReady and decode accept, hold it during backend stalls, flush it on EX redirect/reset, and remove any empty-buffer bypass from io_imemData directly into ID. Keep the existing static backward-branch and direct-JAL next-PC prediction logic on the fetch side.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Treat IF/ID as a registered input. Preserve the existing ID/EX register, decoder legality discipline, immediate generation, and regfile read behavior, but ensure stalls hold the registered instruction cleanly and flushes inject a bubble without depending on live imem data.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Rework frontend backpressure around a registered IF/ID valid/accept protocol instead of raw combinational imem bubbles. Load-use and dmem interlocks still hold younger stages exactly as today; imem not-ready prevents a new fetch entry from being captured rather than forcing decode to sample a NOP from live imem data.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Wire io_imemReady and the new IF/ID accept/valid controls between IF, ID, and the hazard unit while preserving the top-level core IO, the single-retire RVFI channel-0 convention, strict rvfi_order behavior, and tied-off channel 1.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Update IF/ID bundle comments or add small valid/accept helper fields if needed to document the registered fetch/decode boundary. Do not change RVFI widths, architectural state, decoder legality rules, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n================================================================================\nID: hyp-20260428-017-r8s1\nTitle: One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\nCategory: structural\nOutcome: improvement\nFitness: 397.55\nMotivation:\nThe current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\n\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\n\nHypothesis:\n{\n  \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a legal,\\naligned normal-RAM store when io_dmemReady is low. When io_dmemReady is high\\nand the slot is empty, stores drive the existing dmem outputs directly and\\nretire exactly as they do today. When io_dmemReady is low, the MEM stage\\ncaptures the store address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory\\nmetadata into the slot, retires that store once through MEM/WB, and lets\\nyounger non-memory instructions continue while the slot drains.\\n\\nThe slot has priority only while valid. Younger loads, younger stores, and all\\nMMIO/marker/UART stores stall until the pending store drains, preserving\\nin-order memory visibility and exact timing-marker behavior. Misaligned or\\ntrapping stores never enter the slot. This should keep most of the cycle-count\\nbenefit seen in the previous store-buffer experiment, but recover Fmax by\\nleaving the direct ready-store/load path structurally close to the current\\nbest RTL and confining the extra muxing to the stall-only drain path.\\n\",\n  \"expected_impact\": {\n    \"fitness_delta_pct\": 2,\n    \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add a one-entry pending-store slot with valid, enqueue, drain-valid, and\\ndrain-ready controls. The entry holds byte address, replicated write data,\\nand byte mask for legal aligned normal-RAM stores only. It is allocated\\nonly when the live store cannot handshake immediately.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Split store completion into a direct fast path and a low-ready enqueue\\npath. Keep the current dmem outputs and MEM/WB behavior unchanged when\\nio_dmemReady is high and no pending store exists. On a bufferable store\\nwith io_dmemReady low, enqueue the precomputed store fields, retire the\\nstore once with correct RVFI mem_addr, mem_wmask, and mem_wdata, and\\nreport explicit MEM backpressure for loads, nonbufferable stores, stores\\nbehind a valid slot, and full-slot cases.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Replace the generic dmem-stall predicate with MEM-stage wait inputs that\\ndistinguish live loads/nonbufferable stores from a draining pending store.\\nStall IF/ID/EX only when the instruction in MEM truly cannot retire or\\nwhen a younger memory operation must wait behind the slot; allow younger\\nnon-memory instructions to proceed while the slot drains.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Instantiate the pending-store slot and arbitrate the top-level dmem port\\nso the live MEM-stage request uses the direct path whenever the slot is\\nempty, while the slot drives the port only when valid. Preserve the top\\nmodule IO, RVFI NRET=2 fields, strict channel-0 retirement ordering, and\\ntied-off channel 1.\\n\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n================================================================================\n","metadata":{"output":"ID: hyp-20260428-011-r6s1\nTitle: Registered Fetch/Decode Cutpoint\nCategory: structural\nOutcome: regression\nFitness: 330.52\nMotivation:\nThe current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\n\nHypothesis:\n{\n  \"motivation\": \"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID handoff into a real registered pipeline boundary with no zero-latency imem-data-to-decode bypass. IF captures {pc, instr, predicted_taken, predicted_target, valid} when io_imemReady is high and the decode side can accept an entry; ID decodes only the registered entry on the following cycle. Redirects and reset flush the IF/ID register and restart fetch at the resolved target, while load-use and dmem stalls hold the registered entry instead of re-decoding live imem data. The existing backward-branch and direct-JAL prediction still choose the fetch PC in IF, so correctly predicted hot loops and direct jumps should keep one instruction per cycle after fill. The expected win is a shorter critical path and better Fmax; the expected cost is only an extra recovery cycle for residual mispredictions and unpredicted JALR returns, which recent results suggest are smaller than the timing headroom available from cutting the frontend path.\\n\",\n  \"expected_impact\": {\n    \"fitness_delta_pct\": 5,\n    \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID output with an internal IF/ID register. Capture fetched instruction metadata only on io_imemReady and decode accept, hold it during backend stalls, flush it on EX redirect/reset, and remove any empty-buffer bypass from io_imemData directly into ID. Keep the existing static backward-branch and direct-JAL next-PC prediction logic on the fetch side.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Treat IF/ID as a registered input. Preserve the existing ID/EX register, decoder legality discipline, immediate generation, and regfile read behavior, but ensure stalls hold the registered instruction cleanly and flushes inject a bubble without depending on live imem data.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Rework frontend backpressure around a registered IF/ID valid/accept protocol instead of raw combinational imem bubbles. Load-use and dmem interlocks still hold younger stages exactly as today; imem not-ready prevents a new fetch entry from being captured rather than forcing decode to sample a NOP from live imem data.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Wire io_imemReady and the new IF/ID accept/valid controls between IF, ID, and the hazard unit while preserving the top-level core IO, the single-retire RVFI channel-0 convention, strict rvfi_order behavior, and tied-off channel 1.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Update IF/ID bundle comments or add small valid/accept helper fields if needed to document the registered fetch/decode boundary. Do not change RVFI widths, architectural state, decoder legality rules, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n================================================================================\nID: hyp-20260428-017-r8s1\nTitle: One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\nCategory: structural\nOutcome: improvement\nFitness: 397.55\nMotivation:\nThe current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\n\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\n\nHypothesis:\n{\n  \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a legal,\\naligned normal-RAM store when io_dmemReady is low. When io_dmemReady is high\\nand the slot is empty, stores drive the existing dmem outputs directly and\\nretire exactly as they do today. When io_dmemReady is low, the MEM stage\\ncaptures the store address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory\\nmetadata into the slot, retires that store once through MEM/WB, and lets\\nyounger non-memory instructions continue while the slot drains.\\n\\nThe slot has priority only while valid. Younger loads, younger stores, and all\\nMMIO/marker/UART stores stall until the pending store drains, preserving\\nin-order memory visibility and exact timing-marker behavior. Misaligned or\\ntrapping stores never enter the slot. This should keep most of the cycle-count\\nbenefit seen in the previous store-buffer experiment, but recover Fmax by\\nleaving the direct ready-store/load path structurally close to the current\\nbest RTL and confining the extra muxing to the stall-only drain path.\\n\",\n  \"expected_impact\": {\n    \"fitness_delta_pct\": 2,\n    \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add a one-entry pending-store slot with valid, enqueue, drain-valid, and\\ndrain-ready controls. The entry holds byte address, replicated write data,\\nand byte mask for legal aligned normal-RAM stores only. It is allocated\\nonly when the live store cannot handshake immediately.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Split store completion into a direct fast path and a low-ready enqueue\\npath. Keep the current dmem outputs and MEM/WB behavior unchanged when\\nio_dmemReady is high and no pending store exists. On a bufferable store\\nwith io_dmemReady low, enqueue the precomputed store fields, retire the\\nstore once with correct RVFI mem_addr, mem_wmask, and mem_wdata, and\\nreport explicit MEM backpressure for loads, nonbufferable stores, stores\\nbehind a valid slot, and full-slot cases.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Replace the generic dmem-stall predicate with MEM-stage wait inputs that\\ndistinguish live loads/nonbufferable stores from a draining pending store.\\nStall IF/ID/EX only when the instruction in MEM truly cannot retire or\\nwhen a younger memory operation must wait behind the slot; allow younger\\nnon-memory instructions to proceed while the slot drains.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Instantiate the pending-store slot and arbitrate the top-level dmem port\\nso the live MEM-stage request uses the direct path whenever the slot is\\nempty, while the slot drives the port only when valid. Preserve the top\\nmodule IO, RVFI NRET=2 fields, strict channel-0 retirement ordering, and\\ntied-off channel 1.\\n\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n================================================================================\n","exit":0,"description":"Runs Python script to print the full motivation and hypothesis for hyp-20260428-011-r6s1 and hyp-20260428-017-r8s1","truncated":false},"title":"Runs Python script to print the full motivation and hypothesis for hyp-20260428-011-r6s1 and hyp-20260428-017-