{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779282732180,"sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2a2bffepcCr3DVoK1N8UH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"tool_bash_amiZxvjI6b6KCLgW3wga","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Runs Python script to print motivation and hypothesis for hyp-20260428-029-r12s1","command":"python temp_read_load_replay.py"},"output":"ID: hyp-20260428-029-r12s1\nTitle: MEM-Stage Load Replay Buffer\nCategory: structural\nOutcome: regression\nFitness: 442.54\nMotivation:\nThe current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\n\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\nbranch/JAL predictor path and does not add load data to the EX operand muxes.\nThis is a structural stall-hiding change, not a broader predictor or\narithmetic rewrite.\n\nHypothesis:\n{\n  \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\\nbranch/JAL predictor path and does not add load data to the EX operand muxes.\\nThis is a structural stall-hiding change, not a broader predictor or\\narithmetic rewrite.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small normal-RAM load replay buffer in the MEM stage, for example a\\n16-entry direct-mapped table indexed by word address. Each entry holds a\\nvalid bit, word tag, and the raw 32-bit memory word returned by a previous\\nlegal aligned or byte/halfword load. On `io_dmemReady=1`, loads keep using\\nthe live `io_dmemRData` exactly as today and refresh the replay entry for the\\naligned word address. On `io_dmemReady=0`, a legal normal-RAM load may\\ncomplete from the replay buffer on a valid tag hit: suppress the external\\ndmem read, use the cached raw word for sign/zero extension and RVFI\\n`mem_rdata`, and do not assert `mem_wait`.\\n\\nReplay is deliberately conservative. It is disabled while the pending-store\\nslot is valid, for MMIO/marker/UART addresses, for trapping or misaligned\\naccesses, and for stores. Normal-RAM stores invalidate or update the matching\\nreplay entry before they retire or enqueue into the pending-store slot, so a\\nlater replayed load cannot observe stale data. Replay misses preserve the\\ncurrent dmem wait behavior exactly. The top-level IO, single-retire RVFI\\nchannel-0 convention, tied-off channel 1, divider interlock, branch\\nprediction, and pending-store ordering all remain unchanged. The expected win\\nis lower CoreMark cycle count under the 22% random dStall model with much\\nless Fmax risk than MEM-to-EX load forwarding or IF replay caches.\\n\",\n  \"expected_impact\": {\n    \"fitness_delta_pct\": 3,\n    \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/load_replay.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped normal-RAM replay table with valid, tag, and\\nraw-word data state. Provide lookup-hit/data outputs for legal load word\\naddresses, refresh entries from live dmem load responses, and invalidate\\nor byte-merge-update matching entries on legal normal-RAM stores. Reset\\nclears only valid bits.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Integrate the replay lookup into load handling. Select cached raw word\\ndata only when `dmem_ready` is low, the access is a legal non-trapping\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot owns the port, and the replay tag\\nhits. On a replay hit, gate off `dmem_ren`, clear `mem_wait`, drive\\nload-data extraction and RVFI `mem_rdata` from the cached word, and keep\\nreplay misses plus all nonbufferable accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Wire any replay-buffer module instance or MEM-stage sideband signals\\nneeded for store invalidation/update and dmem request gating. Preserve\\nthe existing pending-store-slot arbitration, top-level core IO, RVFI\\nNRET=2 port layout, strict channel-0 order sequence, and tied-off\\nchannel 1 behavior.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if needed to document\\nnormal-RAM replay eligibility and raw-word cache metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n================================================================================\n","metadata":{"output":"ID: hyp-20260428-029-r12s1\nTitle: MEM-Stage Load Replay Buffer\nCategory: structural\nOutcome: regression\nFitness: 442.54\nMotivation:\nT