{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779282748610,"sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2a2bffepcCr3DVoK1N8UH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"glob","callID":"tool_glob_rTlJX8EZGMhR1JsX3Shb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"**/store_slot.sv"},"output":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/v1/rtl/store_slot.sv","metadata":{"count":1,"truncated":false},"title":"","time":{"start":1779282748596,"end":1779282748609}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Discovering the Absence**\n\nI've been digging into `cores/bench/rtl/` and, surprisingly, there's no store buffer or store slot evident. This is quite a finding, especially considering the recent best fitness of 359.04 from `hyp-20260520-003-r1s2`, which was a \"Two-bank registered lookahead instruction replay\" configuration.\n\n**Implementing Store Retirement**\n\nI'm focusing on integrating a one-deep store retirement slot with a direct fast path. Given the absence of a store buffer in the current `cores/bench/rtl/` and the significant performance gains seen in prior hypotheses, this structural improvement appears to be the most promising avenue for further fitness enhancement.\n\n**Exploring Structural Avenues**\n\nI'm re-evaluating the structural hypothesis for `hyp-20260520-002-r2s1`. My focus is on a one-deep store retirement slot with a direct fast path, contrasting it with `hyp-20260428-017-r8s1`'s prior implementation of a pending-store slot. I'm considering if this is the most impactful structural diversity for this round.\n\n**Refining Store Logic**\n\nI'm actively refactoring the memory stage's store completion. My aim is to introduce a split into a direct fast path and a low-ready enqueue path, ensuring existing MEM/WB behavior remains consistent when `io_dmemReady` is high. For bufferable stores when `io_dmemReady` is low, I will enqueue precomputed store fields and retire the store upon receiving correct RVFI memory addresses, masks, and data, while signaling explicit MEM backpressure when necessary.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_glob_rTlJX8EZGMhR1JsX3Shb","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e458424b2001a00n4rX5xgodVW","sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2a2bffepcCr3DVoK1N8UH","messageID":"msg_e458405d6001RCjXlrRPpc0GVa"}}
