{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779282798964,"sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2a2bffepcCr3DVoK1N8UH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"tool_write_iNiItLZRamhiPBUnTtIh","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-002-r2s1.yaml","content":"id: hyp-20260520-002-r2s1\ntitle: \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current 5-stage pipeline in cores/bench/ is heavily bottlenecked by memory backpressure. Under the evaluation's ~22% random data memory stall model, every store instruction that encounters a low dmem_ready signal stalls the entire CPU. However, a store's target address, replicated write data, and byte-lane mask are completely resolved in the MEM stage and require no register destination result. Holding up subsequent non-memory execution during these store bus-busy cycles represents an unnecessary waste of execution bandwidth. A timing-local, one-deep pending-store retirement slot can decouple execute execution from physical store drain latency, hiding bus stalls on store-heavy paths.\nhypothesis: |\n  We propose adding a timing-clean, one-deep pending-store retirement slot to decouple execution from memory stalls:\n  1. Store Slot (store_slot.sv): A small 1-entry buffer holding the word-aligned address, replicated write data, and byte-mask for normal-RAM stores. It is allocated when a legal, aligned store reaches MEM and dmem_ready is low.\n  2. Direct Fast Path: When the store slot is empty and dmem_ready is high, stores drive the dmem outputs directly and retire immediately.\n  3. Precise Backpressure (hazard_unit.sv): Stall IF/ID/EX only when a memory access in MEM truly cannot resolve (e.g. loads, MMIO accesses, or younger stores waiting behind a valid/busy slot). Non-memory instructions (ALU operations and branches) are allowed to bypass and proceed through MEM/WB during active store slot drains.\n  This structural separation of write completion recovers lost execute bandwidth during random bus stalls without introducing additional critical path pressure on the load or fast-store paths.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/store_slot.sv\n    description: \"Implement a one-entry pending-store retirement slot with valid, enqueue, and drain-ready handshakes, capturing the target address, replicated write data, and byte mask of legal, aligned normal-RAM stores.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Incorporate the store slot enqueue logic and split store execution into a direct fast path and a low-ready enqueue path. Generate a precise mem_wait signal to distinguish blockable requests from bufferable stores.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace generic dmem_stall logic with a precise MEM-stage mem_wait signal, allowing the CPU to proceed with younger non-memory instructions while the store slot is draining to memory.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate the store_slot module, arbitrate the top-level dmem port, and wire precise handshake signals between the store slot, MEM stage, and hazard unit, adhering to the single-channel RVFI retirement contract.\"\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-002-r2s1.yaml","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-002-r2s1.yaml","time":{"start":1779282798941,"end":1779282798962}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Refining Hypothesis Structure**\n\nI'm focusing on elegantly structuring our hypothesis, mirroring the established pattern for `cores/bench/rtl/`. I'll also confirm if any other files require adjustment to align with this structure.\n\n**Finalizing Hypothesis Details**\n\nI'm now focusing on the specifics of the hypothesis YAML, particularly refining the `motivation` and `hypothesis` sections. I aim to capture the essence of the one-deep store retirement slot and its direct fast path enablement.\n\n**Implementing Store Slot Logic**\n\nI'm incorporating the one-entry pending-store slot into `store_slot.sv`, ensuring it handles addresses, replicated write data, and byte masks for aligned stores. Simultaneously, `mem_stage.sv` will see the split store completion into fast and enqueue paths, with new `store_slot_enqueue` and `mem_wait` signals controlling stalls for loads and MMIO.\n\n**Adjusting Hazard Unit Logic**\n\nI'm now adapting the hazard unit's stall mechanism, transitioning from the general `dmem_stall` to a more granular `mem_wait` signal from the MEM stage. This change aims to permit non-memory instructions to proceed even as the store buffer drains.\n\n**Clarifying File Formatting**\n\nI'm resolving a discrepancy regarding file formatting within the hypothesis YAML. It appears the schema explicitly requires the `rtl/` prefix for Verilog files, and a `test/` prefix for Python test files. This means `alu.sv` should be `rtl/alu.sv`, aligning with the provided example `rtl/forward_unit.sv`.\n\n**Verifying Schema Constraints**\n\nI'm double-checking the schema, specifically the `expected_impact` object and its `fitness_delta_pct` and `confidence` fields. I'm also confirming the exact file path for hypothesis YAMLs and ensuring the `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/` directory is correctly identified and accessible for writing.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_write_iNiItLZRamhiPBUnTtIh","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e4584e958001RmT3a1QrNe3fzm","sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2a2bffepcCr3DVoK1N8UH","messageID":"msg_e4584b7c6001C0U8aYr8350NZp"}}
