{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779282803030,"sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2a2bffepcCr3DVoK1N8UH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"tool_read_zoVlQp46rN7BHOdcCJOC","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-002-r2s1.yaml"},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-002-r2s1.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260520-002-r2s1\n2: title: \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\"\n3: category: structural\n4: motivation: |\n5:   The current 5-stage pipeline in cores/bench/ is heavily bottlenecked by memory backpressure. Under the evaluation's ~22% random data memory stall model, every store instruction that encounters a low dmem_ready signal stalls the entire CPU. However, a store's target address, replicated write data, and byte-lane mask are completely resolved in the MEM stage and require no register destination result. Holding up subsequent non-memory execution during these store bus-busy cycles represents an unnecessary waste of execution bandwidth. A timing-local, one-deep pending-store retirement slot can decouple execute execution from physical store drain latency, hiding bus stalls on store-heavy paths.\n6: hypothesis: |\n7:   We propose adding a timing-clean, one-deep pending-store retirement slot to decouple execution from memory stalls:\n8:   1. Store Slot (store_slot.sv): A small 1-entry buffer holding the word-aligned address, replicated write data, and byte-mask for normal-RAM stores. It is allocated when a legal, aligned store reaches MEM and dmem_ready is low.\n9:   2. Direct Fast Path: When the store slot is empty and dmem_ready is high, stores drive the dmem outputs directly and retire immediately.\n10:   3. Precise Backpressure (hazard_unit.sv): Stall IF/ID/EX only when a memory access in MEM truly cannot resolve (e.g. loads, MMIO accesses, or younger stores waiting behind a valid/busy slot). Non-memory instructions (ALU operations and branches) are allowed to bypass and proceed through MEM/WB during active store slot drains.\n11:   This structural separation of write completion recovers lost execute bandwidth during random bus stalls without introducing additional critical path pressure on the load or fast-store paths.\n12: expected_impact:\n13:   fitness_delta_pct: 5\n14:   confidence: medium\n15: changes:\n16:   - file: rtl/store_slot.sv\n17:     description: \"Implement a one-entry pending-store retirement slot with valid, enqueue, and drain-ready handshakes, capturing the target address, replicated write data, and byte mask of legal, aligned normal-RAM stores.\"\n18:   - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n19:     description: \"Incorporate the store slot enqueue logic and split store execution into a direct fast path and a low-ready enqueue path. Generate a precise mem_wait signal to distinguish blockable requests from bufferable stores.\"\n20:   - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n21:     description: \"Replace generic dmem_stall logic with a precise MEM-stage mem_wait signal, allowing the CPU to proceed with younger non-memory instructions while the store slot is draining to memory.\"\n22:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n23:     description: \"Instantiate the store_slot module, arbitrate the top-level dmem port, and wire precise handshake signals between the store slot, MEM stage, and hazard unit, adhering to the single-channel RVFI retirement contract.\"\n\n(End of file - total 23 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260520-002-r2s1\ntitle: \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current 5-stage pipeline in cores/bench/ is heavily bottlenecked by memory backpressure. Under the evaluation's ~22% random data memory stall model, every store instruction that encounters a low dmem_ready signal stalls the entire CPU. However, a store's target address, replicated write data, and byte-lane mask are completely resolved in the MEM stage and require no register destination result. Holding up subsequent non-memory execution during these store bus-busy cycles represents an unnecessary waste of execution bandwidth. A timing-local, one-deep pending-store retirement slot can decouple execute execution from physical store drain latency, hiding bus stalls on store-heavy paths.\nhypothesis: |\n  We propose adding a timing-clean, one-deep pending-store retirement slot to decouple execution from memory stalls:\n  1. Store Slot (store_slot.sv): A small 1-entry buffer holding the word-aligned address, replicated write data, and byte-mask for normal-RAM stores. It is allocated when a legal, aligned store reaches MEM and dmem_ready is low.\n  2. Direct Fast Path: When the store slot is empty and dmem_ready is high, stores drive the dmem outputs directly and retire immediately.\n  3. Precise Backpressure (hazard_unit.sv): Stall IF/ID/EX only when a memory access in MEM truly cannot resolve (e.g. loads, MMIO accesses, or younger stores waiting behind a valid/busy slot). Non-memory instructions (ALU operations and branches) are allowed to bypass and proceed through MEM/WB during active store slot drains.\n  This structural separation of write completion recovers lost execute bandwidth during random bus stalls without introducing additional critical path pressure on the load or fast-store paths.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/store_slot.sv\n    description: \"Implement a one-entry pending-store retirement slot with valid, enqueue, and drain-ready handshakes, capturing the target address, replicated write data, and byte mask of legal, aligned normal-RAM stores.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Incorporate the store slot enqueue logic and split store execution into a direct fast path and a low-ready enqueue path. Generate a precise mem_wait signal to distinguish blockable requests from bufferable stores.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-002-r2s1.yaml","time":{"start":1779282802991,"end":1779282803028}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_read_zoVlQp46rN7BHOdcCJOC","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e4584f92700163M6ZB4aCSLw6Y","sessionID":"ses_1ba7d2a2bffepcCr3DVoK1N8UH","messageID":"msg_e4584eaaa001aTfwBkGPzjk5Xu"}}
