{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779280115661,"sessionID":"ses_1baa43bd5ffeTyBXYXMOzEdqBl","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"glob","callID":"tool_glob_t3tf0DEASk5N9fvz1m2j","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl"},"output":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","metadata":{"count":1,"truncated":false},"title":"","time":{"start":1779280115649,"end":1779280115660}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Developing predictor hypothesis**\n\nI am currently exploring architectural hypotheses focused on prediction to enhance the `cores/bench` RV32IM CPU. My primary goal is to boost the CoreMark iter/sec fitness metric, which directly relates to CoreMark iterations per cycle and Fmax on the Tang Nano 20K FPGA. This is my contribution to the diversity slot for this round.\n\n**Analyzing current redirect mechanism**\n\nI've confirmed the baseline RV32IM CPU lacks any explicit branch prediction logic. Branch and jump resolutions currently trigger a redirect in the EX stage, which incurs a 2-cycle penalty due to flushes. My focus is now on how to mitigate this significant performance bottleneck.\n\n**Investigating instruction flow**\n\nI'm analyzing the cycle-by-cycle instruction flow during taken branches. It appears an instruction fetched immediately after the branch target is effectively flushed, even though a new instruction is fetched from the target in the subsequent cycle. I need to pinpoint where this lost instruction's decode is being suppressed.\n\n**Confirming penalty timing**\n\nI'm verifying the exact cycle penalty incurred by taken branches. My current understanding points to a 1-cycle bubble after the branch in the ID stage, not a 2-cycle flush, due to how the pipeline handles instruction fetches immediately following a branch instruction's execution.\n\n**Refining bubble calculation**\n\nMy recent analysis indicates a single-cycle bubble in the EX stage following a taken branch, not a two-cycle flush. This occurs because the instruction fetched immediately after the branch target enters the ID stage in Cycle 3 and can be executed by EX in Cycle 4.\n\n**Investigating Pipeline Flushing**\n\nI'm digging into why both `IF` and `ID` stages are being flushed during taken branches. It appears a wrong-path instruction fetched immediately after the branch is incorrectly discarded, preventing the subsequent correct instruction from being decoded properly.\n\n**Reducing branch penalty**\n\nI'm investigating how to eliminate the single-cycle penalty from taken branches and jumps. By predicting the next PC in the IF stage, I can fetch from the correct target earlier, potentially removing the bubble entirely.\n\n**Searching project for insights**\n\nI'm exploring the project for existing test cases, logs, or other core implementations that might offer guidance on previously attempted branch prediction strategies.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_glob_t3tf0DEASk5N9fvz1m2j","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e455bf7bc001ii6f9Xy2Kokvrv","sessionID":"ses_1baa43bd5ffeTyBXYXMOzEdqBl","messageID":"msg_e455bc4840013lDOxET7sAsN3q"}}
