a.value = 0\n\n  Line 100:     dut.io_imemData.value = imem.get(0, EBREAK())\n\n  Line 150:         dut.io_imemData.value  = imem.get(ia & ~3, EBREAK())\n\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic Fetch Skid Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with an elastic 2-entry fetch skid\\nbuffer that decouples instruction fetch from decode. The fetch side advances\\nthe PC and captures sequential instructions whenever io_imemReady is high\\nand the buffer is not full, even while ID/EX is held by a dmem stall. The\\ndecode side consumes one buffered instruction per non-stalled cycle. Preserve\\nthe baseline zero-latency behavior with an empty-buffer bypass from\\nio_imemData to decode when the backend is ready and imem is ready, so the\\nno-stall path and branch target...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-011-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Registered Fetch/Decode Cutpoint\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the IF/ID handoff into a real registered pipeline boundary with no zero-latency imem-data-to-decode bypass. IF captures {pc, instr, predicted_taken, predicted_target, valid} when io_imemReady is high and the decode side can accept an entry; ID decodes only the registered entry on the following cycle. Redirects and reset flush the IF/ID register and restart fetch at the resolved target, while load-use and dmem stalls hold the re...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-020-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Store-Slot-Aware Fetch Skid Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. The better\\nstructural angle now is narrower: use a tiny fetch skid queue only to absorb\\ninstruction-memory backpressure and to keep fetch productive while the new\\nstore slot is draining for older stores. This targets the measured\\nstore-slot cycle win's remaining companion bottleneck without touching the\\nhot EX operand path, load data forwarding, branch predictor state, or RVFI\\nretirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. T...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-023-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-imem path whenever the external\\nbus is ready, and consult a tiny cached copy only on cycles where imem would\\notherwise stall. CoreMark's timed code repeatedly executes the same matrix,\\nlist, state, CRC, and loop-control instructions over a 10-iteration run, so\\neven a small direct-mapped instruction replay table should turn many random\\niStall bubbles into normal fetch cycles after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-im...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-026-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay table should retain\\nmuch of the iStall hit-rate benefit while reducing the IF storage mux,\\ncomparator fanout, and PC-control load that hurt Fmax in the 256-entry\\nversion. This keeps the zero-wait path direct and targets only cycles that\\nwould otherwise be external imem stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay t...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-027-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Small Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should retain much of the random\\niStall hiding while shortening the IF tag/data muxes that hurt the 256-entry\\nattempt. This stays in the predictor slot because the table predicts the\\ninstruction word for the current PC only when the external fetch bus would\\notherwise provide no word; normal ready fetches still use live imem data.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should reta...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-030-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the cycle savings recovered.\\n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay tables kept much of the same\\ntiming problem while giving up hit rate.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another direction table\\nor return predictor, but a timing-clean replay predictor: keep the large\\niStall-hiding benefit, while moving the table lookup off the same-cycle IF\\ndecision path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the cycle savings recovered.\\n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay table...\n  Line 38: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-031-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Compressed Resetless I-Fetch Replay Tags\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-only cleanups can matter:\\nthe 16-bit segmented RVFI order counter improved Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, while broader datapath rewrites and smaller replay tables\\nlost either hit rate or placement quality. Compressing and de-resetting the\\nexisting replay metadata is the same kind of narrow micro-optimization: keep\\nthe current replay behavior and cycle count, but reduce IF table width,\\nreset fanout, and registered-candidate comparison logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-on...\n  Line 40: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-033-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Two-Word Line I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor result is the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. It kept the large cycle win of the earlier stall-only\\nreplay cache, reducing CoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, while\\nmoving the tag/data lookup off the same-cycle IF decision path enough to\\nreach 472.96 fitness. The predictor experiments after BTFNT and direct JAL\\nshow the useful signal clearly: hiding random imem stalls pays much more\\nthan adding another branch-direction table or return predictor.\\n\\nThe remaining replay loss is likely capacity conflict, not replay timing.\\nThe 64-entry replay attempts gave up many cycles versus the 256-entry\\nversion, so hot text footprint and aliasing matter. Simply doubling a\\nfull-tag word table risks growing the registered lookup mux and tag storage\\nin the IF stage. A better predictor-shaped next step is to pack adjacent\\ninstruction words under one line tag: it increases effective replay\\ncapacity for sequential CoreMark code while amortizing the full PC tag over\\ntwo instructions and preserving the current registered-candidate timing\\nmodel.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor result is the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. It kept the large cycle win of the earlier stall-only\\nreplay cache, reducing CoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, while\\nmoving the tag/data lookup off the same-cycle IF decision path enough to\\nreach 472.96 fitness. The predictor experiments after BTFNT and direct JAL\\nshow the useful signal clearly: hiding random imem stalls pays much more\\nthan adding another branch-direction table or return predictor.\\n\\nThe remaining replay loss is likely capacity conflict, not replay timing.\\nThe 64-entry replay attempts gave up many cycles versus the 256-entry\\nversion, so hot text footprint and aliasing matter. Simply doubling a\\nful...\n  Line 46: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-039-r15s2\", \"title\": \"Registered I-Fetch Replay Victim Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is driven by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor with compressed, resetless tags. That predictor keeps the\\nlarge random-iStall cycle reduction of the 256-entry replay table while\\navoiding a same-cycle table read on the IF decision path. The experiment log\\nalso shows the boundary clearly: smaller replay tables gave up too much hit\\nrate, two-word replay lines recovered only a little cycle count while losing\\nFmax, and branch-direction or return predictors have consistently lost more\\nclock than their control-flow savings recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are therefore most likely direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses in CoreMark's hot text footprint, not a lack of branch-direction\\nsophistication. A tiny victim predictor behind the existing 256-entry replay\\ntable targets those conflicts without widening the main table, adding a new\\nbranch update path, or putting a combinational lookup back onto current-cycle\\nfetch_ready.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is driven by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor with compressed, resetless tags. That predictor keeps the\\nlarge random-iStall cycle reduction of the 256-entry replay table while\\navoiding a same-cycle table read on the IF decision path. The experiment log\\nalso shows the boundary clearly: smaller replay tables gave up too much hit\\nrate, two-word replay lines recovered only a little cycle count while losing\\nFmax, and branch-direction or return predictors have consistently lost more\\nclock than their control-flow savings recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are therefore most likely direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses in CoreMark's hot text footprint, not a lack of branch-direction\\nsophistication. A tiny victim predictor behind the existing 256-entry replay\\ntable targets those conflicts without w...\n  Line 47: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-040-r16s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay Candidate Payloads\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result is built around the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. The strongest recent micro-opt win came from compressing\\nreplay tags and making replay table payload arrays resetless behind valid\\nbits; broader attempts to remove the replay candidate PC guard or apply\\nvalid-only reset discipline across the whole pipeline regressed. That points\\nto a narrower follow-up in the same proven area.\\n\\n`if_stage` still synchronously resets the registered replay candidate PC and\\ninstruction payload to `RESET_PC` and `NOP`, even though those fields are\\nconsumed only when `replay_cand_valid_q` is asserted and the existing full-PC\\nguard matches the current fetch PC. Reset muxes and reset fanout on these\\n64 payload bits sit next to the IF replay/fetch-ready path that now matters\\nmost for Fmax. Clearing only the candidate valid bit should preserve the\\ncurrent replay hit rate and all visible behavior while trimming a small but\\ntiming-relevant reset structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is built around the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. The strongest recent micro-opt win came from compressing\\nreplay tags and making replay table payload arrays resetless behind valid\\nbits; broader attempts to remove the replay candidate PC guard or apply\\nvalid-only reset discipline across the whole pipeline regressed. That points\\nto a narrower follow-up in the same proven area.\\n\\n`if_stage` still synchronously resets the registered replay candidate PC and\\ninstruction payload to `RESET_PC` and `NOP`, even though those fields are\\nconsumed only when `replay_cand_valid_q` is asserted and the existing full-PC\\nguard matches the current fetch PC. Reset muxes and reset fanout on these\\n64 payload bits sit next to the IF replay/fetch-ready path that now matters\\nmost for Fmax. Clearing only ...\n  Line 49: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-042-r16s2\", \"title\": \"Replay-Cached Next-PC Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It keeps the large random-iStall cycle\\nwin of the 256-entry replay cache while avoiding a same-cycle table lookup\\non the IF decision path. Later predictor experiments show that adding new\\nbranch-direction tables, return predictors, victim entries, or wider replay\\nlines either saves very few cycles or loses more Fmax than it recovers.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor work is therefore inside the replay path\\nthat already wins. On a replay hit, the IF stage currently feeds the cached\\ninstruction word back through the same static branch/JAL decode and target\\nadders used for live imem data. That means an external imem stall served by\\nreplay still has a flop-to-flop path from `replay_cand_instr_q` through\\nbranch/JAL immediate reconstruction, target adders, the