 adding ways or\\nvictim entries added too much logic. A hashed direct-map index targets the\\nsame conflict misses while keeping the table size, registered lookahead\\ntiming model, live-imem-first policy, and compressed tag storage intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tabl...\n  Line 61: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-054-r20s2\", \"title\": \"Banked Registered I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best remaining replay opportunity is to reduce direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses without adding an associative lookup or changing the current-cycle\\nfetch-ready path. A full victim buffer added several tag compares and\\nregressed. XOR indexing changed conflicts but did not improve the measured\\nhit-rate/Fmax product. A banked direct-mapped predictor is a narrower\\nalternative: keep two small direct-mapped replay banks and choose between\\nthem with a simple per-set MRU bit during registered lookahead generation.\\nThe current cycle still consumes only the already registered replay\\ncandidate, so any extra bank selection work stays off the IF replay-hit path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best...\n  Line 62: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-055-r21s0\", \"title\": \"Bank-Aware Replay Fill Select\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses more Fmax\\nthan it recovers, so the next micro-opt should keep the successful two-bank\\norganization and trim redundant logic inside it.\\n\\nThe current banked replay table still does two same-cycle tag comparisons for\\nevery live imem fill:\\n`replay_fill_bank0_match` and `replay_fill_bank1_match` re-read the current\\nPC's indexed set to decide whether to refresh bank 0, refresh bank 1, or\\nreplace the non-MRU bank. But the registered lookahead candidate for the\\ncurrent fetch PC was produced by comparing exactly those two banks on the\\nprevious cycle. If that candidate hits and its PC guard matches the current\\nPC, it already identifies that the word is present and which bank supplied\\nit. If it misses, the previous lookup already proved that neither bank held\\nthe current tag, so replacement can use the non-MRU bank directly.\\n\\nReusing the registered candidate's bank for live fills should remove two\\ncurrent-PC tag compare trees and part of the fill-bank muxing from the IF\\nreplay block while keeping the current replay hit rate, table size, MRU\\npolicy, candidate PC guard, and live-imem-first behavior intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses ...\n  Line 65: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-058-r22s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay MRU State\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for correctness after reset: both replay-bank valid bits are cleared,\\nso the first live fill for a set may choose either bank. After that fill, the\\nMRU bit is immediately written to the bank that was filled and normal\\nreplacement behavior resumes.\\n\\nRemoving reset from this 128-bit replacement-state array is a narrower\\nversion of the reset-elision idea that has already worked for replay\\npayloads. It targets reset fanout and local IF replay placement pressure\\nwithout changing the current-cycle fetch-ready path, replay hit rate after\\nwarmup, branch/JAL prediction policy, ALU datapath, memory behavior, or RVFI\\nretirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for ...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/v1/rtl/core.sv:\n  Line 27:   input  logic [31:0] io_imemData,\n\n  Line 144:     .imem_data       (io_imemData),\n\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:\n  Line 86:     dut.io_imemData.value = 0\n\n  Line 97:     dut.io_imemData.value = imem.get(0, EBREAK())\n\n  Line 147:         dut.io_imemData.value  = imem.get(ia & ~3, EBREAK())\n\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/baseline/test/test_pipeline.py:\n  Line 86:     dut.io_imemData.value = 0\n\n  Line 97:     dut.io_imemData.value = imem.get(0, EBREAK())\n\n  Line 147:         dut.io_imemData.value  = imem.get(ia & ~3, EBREAK())\n\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/rtl/core.sv:\n  Line 28:   input  logic [31:0] io_imemData,\n\n  Line 106:     .imem_data       (io_imemData),\n\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/baseline/rtl/core.sv:\n  Line 27:   input  logic [31:0] io_imemData,\n\n  Line 127:     .imem_data       (io_imemData),\n\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/bench/kimi-k2_6/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"2-entry return-address stack for JALR prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline pays a 2-cycle flush bubble for every taken JALR (function return).\\nCoreMark is call-heavy, so returns are a significant fraction of dynamic control flow.\\nPrior BTB attempts either thrashed (8-entry) or were synthesized away (implementation bugs),\\nand static BTFN predictors destroyed Fmax by adding an imem_data->adder->next_pc critical path.\\nA tiny 2-entry RAS predicts returns with near-perfect accuracy, avoids an IF-stage adder entirely,\\nand targets a different instruction class (returns vs branches) than all previous predictor attempts.\\nCorrectly predicted returns eliminate the flush bubble, directly improving IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline pays a 2-cycle flush bubble for every taken JALR (function return).\\nCoreMark is call-heavy, so returns are a significant fraction of dynamic control flow.\\nPrior BTB attempts either thrashed (8-entry) or were synthesized away (implementation bugs),\\nand static BTFN predictors destroyed Fmax by adding an imem_data->adder->next_pc critical path.\\nA tiny 2-entry RAS predicts returns with near-perfect accuracy, avoids an IF-stage adder entirely,\\nand targets a different instruction class (returns vs branches) than all previous predictor attempts.\\nCorrectly predicted returns eliminate the flush bubble, directly improving IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 2-entry Return Address Stack (RAS) in core.sv and change EX-stage redirect to\\nmispredict-only so that correct return predictions do not flush the pipeline.\\n\\n1. RAS logic in core.sv (top level, no new module):\\n   - Detect push:  jal with rd==ra  (opcode 1101111, instr[11:7]==1).\\n   - Detect pop/predict: jalr with rs1==ra (opcode 1100111, instr[19:15]==1).\\n   - Two 32-bit registers (ras_top, ras_next) plus pointer implied by the swap.\\n   - On push: ras_next <= ras_top; ras_top <= io_imemAddr + 32'd4.\\n   - On ...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"ICache-integrated JAL and backward-branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline flushes IF/ID on every taken JAL and backward branch, costing 2 cycles each.\\nPrior predictor attempts failed for three reasons: an 8-entry standalone BTB thrashed on\\nCoreMark's many active sites (0% IPC gain), a 2-entry RAS decoded io_imemData in core.sv\\nand fed it back to IF (Fmax crashed to 70 MHz), and a BTFN static predictor broke\\nrvfi_pc_fwd by altering EX-stage pc_next.  The existing 8-entry I-cache already stores\\ninstructions indexed by PC with tag match.  Extending each entry with a single valid bit\\nand a 32-bit target reuses the cache's fast read path, stays entirely inside IF, avoids\\nany combinational decode of external imemData, and costs only ~50 LUT4s.  By predicting\\nonly unconditional JAL and backward branches\\u2014whose targets are stable and highly\\nrepetitive in loop-heavy CoreMark\\u2014we avoid the aliasing thrashing that neutralized the\\nprior BTB, while mispredict-only redirect keeps EX's architectural pc_next completely\\nunchanged, preserving rvfi_pc_fwd.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline flushes IF/ID on every taken JAL and backward branch, costing 2 cycles each.\\nPrior predictor attempts failed for three reasons: an 8-entry standalone BTB thrashed on\\nCoreMark's many active sites (0% IPC gain), a 2-entry RAS decoded io_imemData in core.sv\\nand fed it back to IF (Fmax crashed to 70 MHz), and a BTFN static predictor broke\\nrvfi_pc_fwd by altering EX-stage pc_next.  The existing 8-entry I-cache already stores\\ninstructions indexed by PC with tag match.  Extending each entry with a single valid bit\\nand a 32-bit target reuses the cache's fast read path, stays entirely inside IF, avoids\\nany combinational decode of external imemData, and costs only ~50 LUT4s.  By predicting\\nonly unconditional JAL and backward branches\\u2014whose targets are stable and highly\\nrepetitive in loop-he...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/bench/kimi-k2_6/rep3/agent.log:\n  Line 157: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778766903421,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d93f6699ffeYnz42SWvDsIkwd\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"read:12\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/kimi-k2_6-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\"},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/kimi-k2_6-rep3/cores/bench/rtl/core.sv</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: // rtl/core.sv\\n2: //\\n3: // Top-level wiring for the 5-stage in-order RV32IM core.\\n4: //\\n5: //  IF -> ID -> EX -> MEM -> WB\\n6: //   |    |     ^\\n7: //   |    +-----+    forward_unit drives EX-stage rs1/rs2 muxes\\n8: //   |               from EX/MEM and MEM/WB\\n9: //   +- stall <- hazard_unit (load-use)\\n10: //\\n11: // IO port names use the `io_*` Chisel-emit prefix so the existing\\n12: // formal/wrapper_si.sv and test/cosim/main.cpp bindings carry through\\n13: // byte-for-byte. RVFI port set is the single-channel set described in\\n14: // CLAUDE.md invariant 1 under `nret: 1` (declared in core.yaml). The\\n15: // orchestrator routes formal to wrapper_si.sv + checks_si.cfg and FPGA\\n16: // synth to fpga/core_bench_si.sv for this core. There is no channel 1.\\n17: //\\n18: // Latency:        full pipeline; instruction n retires at MEM/WB on\\n19: //                 cycle n+4 (no hazards) or later (load-use stall,\\n20: //                 redirect).\\n21: // RVFI fields:    all of them — driven from the MEM/WB register and\\n22: //                 the WB-stage write-data mux.\\n23: module core (\\n24:   input  logic        clock,\\n25:   input  logic        reset,\\n26:   // imem\\n27:   output logic [31:0] io_imemAddr,\\n28:   input  logic [31:0] io_imemData,\\n29:   // imem bus backpressure. Drive 1 for zero-wait single-cycle BRAM (the\\n30:   // V0 default). Drive 0 to model bus stall — PC reg holds, IF/ID\\n31:   // payload becomes a NOP, and a pipeline bubble propagates downstream.\\n32:   // Used by test/cosim/vex_main.cpp's ...\n  Line 160: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778766951169,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d93f6699ffeYnz42SWvDsIkwd\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"read:13\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/kimi-k2_6-rep3/fpga/core_bench_si.sv\"},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/kimi-k2_6-rep3/fpga/core_bench_si.sv</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: // fpga/core_bench_si.sv\\n2: //\\n3: // FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\\n4: // core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\\n5: // set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\\n6: // synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\\n7: //\\n8: // See core_bench.sv for the full rationale on LFSR-driven imem, dmem\\n9: // model, and the XOR-reduce LED that keeps RVFI fan-out alive through\\