PC mux lengthen the fetch path more than CoreMark branch-bubble savings recover.\\n6: - 2026-05-12 hyp-20260512-001-r3s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use bits for load-use ...\n  Line 395: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778629370260,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1e16dcefcffe3b0eWaieghB464\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_pRtU4T9qcqaPPp1Z2llViyuw\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"category.*structural|structural|Register IF/ID|pipeline|stage|retim|EX/MEM|MEM/WB|split|collapse|bubble|valid\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 27 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_5_xhigh-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260512-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Move DIV/REM off ALU critical path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current one-cycle ALU contains real SystemVerilog division and remainder\\\\noperators in the same EX-stage result cone as common integer operations.\\\\nThose operators are expensive on the Tang Nano 20K fabric and likely depress\\\\nFmax, while CoreMark's timed region should execute DIV/REM rarely or not at\\\\nall. Improving Fmax without touching the common ADD/branch/load/store path is\\\\ntherefore a promising fitness win.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current one-cycle ALU contains real SystemVerilog division and remainder\\\\noperators in the same EX-stage result cone as common integer operations.\\\\nThose operators are expensive on the Tang Nano 20K fabric and likely depress\\\\nFmax, while CoreMark's timed region should execute DIV/REM rarely or not at\\\\nall. Improving Fmax without touching the common ADD/branch/load/store path is\\\\ntherefore a promising fitness win.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU into a small bounded\\\\niterative divider. The in-order pipeline should stall only when one of those\\\\nrare operations is in EX, then write the completed result through the existing\\\\nEX/MEM path. Keep MUL/MULH variants one-cycle and keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\\\nsubstituti...\n\n(Results truncated: showing 100 of 338 matches (238 hidden). Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)","metadata":{"matches":338,"truncated":true},"title":"io_imemData","time":{"start":1779280141616,"end":1779280141675}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Implementing Static Branch Prediction**\n\nI'm currently exploring the possibility of incorporating a static branch predictor similar to Ibex's. My focus is on replicating its logic for jump instructions and conditional branches based on PC offset direction.\n\n**Analyzing Instruction Fetch Behavior**\n\nI'm looking into how instruction memory data becomes available. It appears `imem_data` is returned combinationally based on `imem_addr`, but I need to confirm if memory access is truly single-cycle, especially considering potential stalls in cosim mode.\n\n**Confirming Memory Timing**\n\nI'm wrestling with the exact timing of instruction memory reads. My current thinking suggests that for synchronous BRAM, the address presented in cycle N yields data in cycle N+1. This implies `imem_addr` must be captured the cycle *before* it's used by the IF stage. I'm digging into the Verilog to confirm this dependency.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_grep_3wuXRHE1Sy4CHCkkFAG1","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e455c5d25001l1f59bYdyV7A3s","sessionID":"ses_1baa43bd5ffeTyBXYXMOzEdqBl","messageID":"msg_e455c4134001dbPzU86fCBwNnM"}}
