pothesis\": \"Add a small non-speculative return-address stack for link-register control\\nflow. When a valid non-trapping JAL or JALR call with rd=x1 or rd=x5 advances\\nthrough EX, push pc+4 into a 4-entry stack. When a valid canonical return\\nJALR with rd=x0, rs1=x1 or rs1=x5, funct3=000, and imm=0 advances through EX,\\npop one entry. In IF, recognize the same canonical return instruction and,\\nwhen the stack is non-empty, predict the next PC as the stack top using the\\nexisting predicted_taken/predicted_target metadata path. Change JALR redirect\\ngeneration in EX to the same resolved-next-PC mismatch check used for\\npredicted JALs: unpredicted or incorrectly predicted JA...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-009-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny Forward-Branch Override Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, reaching 324.48 fitness. The 64-entry agree\\npredictor experiment showed that residual conditional-branch misses still\\nmatter: it reduced CoreMark cycles from 4,259,699 to 4,194,484, but the\\nlarger always-consulted table cost enough Fmax to regress overall fitness.\\nThat result points to a narrower predictor: keep the proven BTFNT and direct\\nJAL behavior intact, but learn only the static predictor's most expensive\\nblind spot, biased forward conditional branches. A tiny forward-only override\\nshould retain much of the cycle-count gain with far less table area, fanout,\\nand IF routing pressure than the full agree predictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, reaching 324.48 fitness. The 64-entry agree\\npredictor experiment showed that residual conditional-branch misses still\\nmatter: it reduced CoreMark cycles from 4,259,699 to 4,194,484, but the\\nlarger always-consulted table cost enough Fmax to regress overall fitness.\\nThat result points to a narrower predictor: keep the proven BTFNT and direct\\nJAL behavior intact, but learn only the static predictor's most expensive\\nblind spot, biased forward conditional branches. A tiny forward-only override\\nshould retain much of the cycle-count gain with far less table area, fanout,\\nand IF routing pressure than the full agree predictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 16-entry PC-indexed two-bit saturating override table that is consulted\\nonly for legal, aligned, forward B-type conditional branches. Backward\\nbranches keep the current static taken prediction, and direct JAL prediction\\nremains unchanged. Forward branches cold-start as strongly not-taken so the\\nreset behavior exactly matches the current...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-012-r6s2\", \"title\": \"experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260428-012-r6s2.yaml\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"schema_error: 1.5 is not of type 'integer'\\n\\nFailed validating 'type' in schema['properties']['expected_impact']['properties']['fitness_delta_pct']:\\n    {'type': 'integer'}\\n\\nOn instance['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']:\\n    1.5\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor stack is static backward-branch taken plus IF\\ndirect-JAL prediction. Attempts to add broader learned conditional-branch\\npredictors improved CoreMark cycle count but lost more Fmax than they gained,\\nwhile the 4-entry RAS also regressed because it added stack state, push/pop\\nbookkeeping, and enough IF routing to lower median Fmax. The useful signal\\nfrom the RAS experiment is that returns exist in the timed path, but the\\npredictor must be much narrower and cheaper than a general stack.\\n\\nA one-entry last-return target predictor targets the same remaining JALR\\nhole with less state and less control. CoreMark's hot call/return behavior is\\ntypically dominated by repeated returns from stable call sites, so a single\\ncached canonical-return target can remove many return flushes without a BTB,\\nbranch table, multi-entry stack, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry return target cache in IF. When EX resolves a valid,\\nnon-trapping canonical return JALR (`jalr x0, 0(x1)` or `jalr x0, 0(x5)`),\\nrecord the return instruction PC and its resolved next PC. When IF later\\nfetches the same canonical return PC and the entry is valid, predict the\\ncached target through the existing `predicted_taken` / `predicted_target`\\nmetadata path. Change JALR redirect generation to the same resolved-next-PC\\nmismatch check used for predicted JALs, so correctly predicted cached returns\\ndo not flush and all other JALRs retain the current EX re...\n  Line 22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-015-r7s2\", \"title\": \"One-Entry Return Target Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, and the latest winning change moved cold DIV/REM\\noff the critical ALU path. The predictor slot should therefore avoid broad\\nlearned branch tables that have already shown a bad cycle-count versus Fmax\\ntradeoff: the 64-entry agree table and 16-entry forward override both reduced\\nCoreMark cycles but lost more clock than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining high-value predictor miss is JALR return control flow. The\\n4-entry RAS experiment showed returns exist in the timed path, but its stack\\nbookkeeping and IF routing were still too expensive. A single tagged return\\ntarget entry is a narrower bet: it targets recurring canonical return PCs\\nwith one PC tag, one target register, and one IF compare, avoiding a BTB,\\nmulti-entry stack, or conditional-branch table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, and the latest winning change moved cold DIV/REM\\noff the critical ALU path. The predictor slot should therefore avoid broad\\nlearned branch tables that have already shown a bad cycle-count versus Fmax\\ntradeoff: the 64-entry agree table and 16-entry forward override both reduced\\nCoreMark cycles but lost more clock than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining high-value predictor miss is JALR return control flow. The\\n4-entry RAS experiment showed returns exist in the timed path, but its stack\\nbookkeeping and IF routing were still too expensive. A single tagged return\\ntarget entry is a narrower bet: it targets recurring canonical return PCs\\nwith one PC tag, one target register, and one IF compare, avoiding a BTB,\\nmulti-entry stack, or conditional-branch table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry return target predictor for canonical return JALRs. When EX\\nresolves a valid...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-018-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Funct3-Biased Static Branch Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already has the two predictor features that have\\nactually paid off: backward conditional branches predict taken, and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned predictor attempts point in the\\nother direction: both the 64-entry agree table and the 16-entry forward-only\\noverride table reduced CoreMark cycles, but their table read/update routing\\ncost more Fmax than the saved mispredicts recovered. Return prediction was\\neven less attractive after the post-DIV timing win, with only small cycle\\nsavings and a large clock regression.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor angle is therefore a zero-state refinement\\nof the static conditional-branch rule. A quick dynamic profile of the timed\\nCoreMark region shows that the baseline BTFNT rule is especially wrong for\\ntwo simple branch classes: backward BEQ branches, which are mostly not taken\\nin this binary, and forward BNE branches, which are usually taken. Those two\\nclasses account for a large fraction of residual static predictor mistakes,\\nand they can be recognized from the funct3 bits already examined in IF for\\nbranch legality. This targets iterations/cycle without adding a predictor\\nRAM, update feedback path, BTB, RAS, or extra target adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already has the two predictor features that have\\nactually paid off: backward conditional branches predict taken, and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned predictor attempts point in the\\nother direction: both the 64-entry agree table and the 16-entry forward-only\\noverride table reduced CoreMark cycles, but their table read/update routing\\ncost more Fmax than the saved mispredicts recovered. Return prediction was\\neven less attractive after the post-DIV timing win, with only small cycle\\nsavings and a large clock regression.\\n\\nThe remaining p...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-021-r9s2\", \"title\": \"X1 Last-Call Return Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already has the predictor features that have proven\\nuseful without excessive timing cost: backward conditional branches are\\npredicted taken and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF. Broader learned\\ndirection tables did reduce CoreMark cycles, but the IF table read and EX\\nupdate routing cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. The return\\npredictor attempts also showed that return bubbles exist, but the 4-entry\\nstack and the PC-tagged one-entry cache added too much frontend comparison,\\nbookkeeping, and routing.\\n\\nThe narrow remaining predictor opportunity is to keep only the useful part\\nof a return-address stack: the standard ABI link register x1. CoreMark is\\ncompiled as ordinary RV32IM C code, so direct calls write x1 and returns are\\ncanonical `jalr x0, 0(x1)`. A single last-call target register can predict\\nthe common innermost return without a BTB, tag compare, multi-entry stack, or\\nconditional-branch table. It intentionally gives up nested-return coverage in\\nexchange for a much smaller IF critical-path footprint.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already has the predictor features that have proven\\nuseful without excessive timing cost: backward conditional branches are\\npredicted taken and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF. Broader learned\\ndirection tables did reduce CoreMark cycles, but the IF table read and EX\\nupdate routing cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. The return\\npredictor attempts also showed that return bubbles exist, but the 4-entry\\nstack and the PC-tagged one-entry cache added too much frontend comparison,\\nbookkeeping, and routing.\\n\\nThe narrow remaining predictor opportunity is to keep only the useful part\\nof a return-address stack: the standard ABI link register x1. CoreMark is\\ncompiled as ordinary RV32IM C code, so direct calls write x1 and r...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-024-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Adder Static Control Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor features are the simple ones that already paid\\nfor themselves: backward conditional branches are predicted taken and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned direction tables and return\\npredictors reduced CoreMark cycles slightly, but each added enough IF-state,\\ncomparison, or EX-to-IF update routing to lose more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to make the profitable\\nstatic predictor cheaper rather than adding more prediction state. Today\\n`if_stage` forms branch and JAL targets with two separate `pc + imm` adders\\nand uses the full adder results for target-alignment gating before choosing\\nthe next PC. Since B- and J-type immediates always have bit 0 clear, the\\ntarget alignment test can be derived from the encoded imm[1] bit and the PC\\nlow bits without waiting for a 32-bit target adder. Branch and JAL are also\\nmutually exclusive opcodes, so one selected-immediate target adder is enough.\\n\\nThis attacks predictor-path Fmax with no intended CoreMark cycle-count risk:\\nthe set of predicted conditional branches and direct JALs remains the same\\nas the current best design for all aligned PCs, and EX still repairs any\\nwrong prediction through the existing resolved-next-PC mismatch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor features are the simple ones that already paid\\nfor themselves: backward conditional branches are predicted taken and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned direction tables and return\\npredictors reduced CoreMark cycles slightly, but each added enough IF-state,\\ncomparison, or EX-to-IF update routing to lose more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to make the profitable\\nstatic predictor cheaper rather than adding more prediction state. Today\\n`if_stage` forms bra...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-027-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Small Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor changes that have paid so far are the ones that avoid broad\\nlearned direction state: backward conditional branches and direct JAL targets\\nare predicted in IF, while the larger branch tables and return predictors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay pr