n   extract the B/J immediate from imem_data. Detect opcode 1100011\\n   (BRANCH) and 1101111 (JAL). Predict taken for JAL unconditionally,\\n   and for branches when the immediate sign bit is 1 (backward branch).\\n   On a predicted-taken instruction, redirect next_pc...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"32-entry direct-mapped BTB in IF stage to eliminate taken-branch bubbles\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches sequentially, paying a 2-cycle bubble for every\\ntaken branch and JAL. A prior static predictor that computed branch targets\\ncombinationally in IF from imem_data (decode -> imm_gen -> adder) slashed\\nFmax by ~40% because that path starts from the off-chip/BRAM instruction\\nword. A Branch Target Buffer (BTB) avoids that path entirely: it is indexed\\ndirectly by the PC register output, so the predicted next PC is just a small\\ndistributed-RAM read with no instruction decode or adder in the critical\\npath. On the Tang Nano 20K a 32-entry BTB costs only ~35 LUTs and reads in\\na single LUT delay, making it a much safer timing bet while still removing\\nthe branch bubble for loop-back edges once they warm up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches sequentially, paying a 2-cycle bubble for every\\ntaken branch and JAL. A prior static predictor that computed branch targets\\ncombinationally in IF from imem_data (decode -> imm_gen -> adder) slashed\\nFmax by ~40% because that path starts from the off-chip/BRAM instruction\\nword. A Branch Target Buffer (BTB) avoids that path entirely: it is indexed\\ndirectly by the PC register output, so the predicted next PC is just a small\\ndistributed-RAM read with no instruction decode or adder in the critical\\npath. On the Tang Nano 20K a 32-entry BTB costs only ~35 LUTs and reads in\\na single LUT delay, making it a much safer timing bet while still removing\\nthe branch bubble for loop-back edges once they warm up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 32-entry direct-mapped BTB to the IF stage.\\n- Read: combinational, indexed by pc[6:2], outputs hit and target[31:0].\\n- Write: registered in EX when a conditional branch or JAL is resolved\\n  taken, storing pc[6:2] -> target.\\n- IF stage: next_pc = redirect ? redirect_target : btb_hit ? btb_target : ...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"64-entry tagged BTB inlined in IF with mispredict-only redirect\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Dynamic profiling shows CoreMark executes ~360k taken control-flow\\ninstructions (branches + JAL + JALR) out of 2.9M total instructions.\\nThe baseline always predicts not-taken, paying a 2-cycle bubble for\\nevery one of them.  Eliminating these bubbles would cut ~718k cycles\\nfrom the 4.5M-cycle run, yielding a ~16% IPC improvement.\\n\\nA prior 32-entry direct-mapped BTB attempt produced exactly zero\\nfitness delta with identical LUT/Fmax numbers, strongly suggesting the\\nBTB was synthesized away because the update path or the next-pc mux\\nwas not wired correctly.  This hypothesis inlines the BTB directly\\ninto if_stage.sv (no separate module to forget to instantiate), adds\\na partial tag to avoid aliasing, and explicitly spells out the exact\\nnext-pc expression and the pc+4 mispredict-recovery path so the\\nimplementation agent cannot leave it as a no-op.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Dynamic profiling shows CoreMark executes ~360k taken control-flow\\ninstructions (branches + JAL + JALR) out of 2.9M total instructions.\\nThe baseline always predicts not-taken, paying a 2-cycle bubble for\\nevery one of them.  Eliminating these bubbles would cut ~718k cycles\\nfrom the 4.5M-cycle run, yielding a ~16% IPC improvement.\\n\\nA prior 32-entry direct-mapped BTB attempt produced exactly zero\\nfitness delta with identical LUT/Fmax numbers, strongly suggesting the\\nBTB was synthesized away because the update path or the next-pc mux\\nwas not wired correctly.  This hypothesis inlines the BTB directly\\ninto if_stage.sv (no separate module to forget to instantiate), adds\\na partial tag to avoid aliasing, and explicitly spells out the exact\\nnext-pc expression and the pc+4 mispredict-recovery path so the\\nimplementation agent cannot leave it as a no-op.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Inline a 64-entry direct-mapped branch-target buffer into the IF ...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"16-entry inlined BTB with explicit RVFI-safe mispredict-only redirect\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline pays a 2-cycle bubble for every taken branch and JAL because\\nit always fetches PC+4 and redirects only after EX resolution. Prior\\nattempts to add a BTB either produced zero fitness delta (identical LUT/FF\\ncounts prove the structure was synthesized away because the update path was\\ndisconnected) or broke rvfi_pc_fwd (the implementation agent altered\\nEX/MEM.pc_next to depend on redirect, so a correct prediction reported\\nPC+4 instead of the actual target). A very small, explicitly inlined BTB\\navoids module-instantiation mistakes, and spelling out the exact combinational\\nchanges in ex_stage.sv with a bold \\u201cdo not touch pc_next\\u201d rule should finally\\nlet the predictor actually fire without corrupting the architectural trace.\\nCoreMark is loop-heavy; even a 16-entry table should capture the handful of\\nhot backward edges and JAL targets that dominate dynamic control flow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline pays a 2-cycle bubble for every taken branch and JAL because\\nit always fetches PC+4 and redirects only after EX resolution. Prior\\nattempts to add a BTB either produced zero fitness delta (identical LUT/FF\\ncounts prove the structure was synthesized away because the update path was\\ndisconnected) or broke rvfi_pc_fwd (the implementation agent altered\\nEX/MEM.pc_next to depend on redirect, so a correct prediction reported\\nPC+4