on any\\nJALR return (rd = x0, rs1 = x1/x5). Thread the predicted target through a\\nnew `pred_target` field in the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles. In EX stage,\\ncompare the actual JALR target with `pred_target`; only redirect on\\nmispredict or when JALR was not predicted. This eliminates the redirect\\nbubble on correct function returns.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `pred_target` field to `if_id_t` and `id_ex_t` structs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add 4-entry RAS with push/pop control; use RAS top as predicted target for JALR returns; drive `out.pred_target`.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread `in.pred_target` into the ID/EX register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace unconditional JALR redirect with mispredict detection using `in....\n  Line 40: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Hash-mix higher PC bits into 32-entry BHT index to reduce aliasing\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The 32-entry 2-bit saturating-counter BHT is the proven sweet spot for\\nthis core, delivering a +7.3% fitness gain. A follow-up attempt to grow\\nthe table to 128 entries regressed -3% because the wider read mux hurt\\nFmax, while gshare global-history variants regressed due to history\\nstaleness. The remaining occasional forward-branch mispredictions may\\nstem from destructive aliasing among branches that share the low five\\nPC bits. A zero-cost hash that XORs higher PC bits into the index\\nscatters colliding branches without enlarging the array or adding any\\nnew state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 32-entry 2-bit saturating-counter BHT is the proven sweet spot for\\nthis core, delivering a +7.3% fitness gain. A follow-up attempt to grow\\nthe table to 128 entries regressed -3% because the wider read mux hurt\\nFmax, while gshare global-history variants regressed due to history\\nstaleness. The remaining occasional forward-branch mispredictions may\\nstem from destructive aliasing among branches that share the low five\\nPC bits. A zero-cost hash that XORs higher PC bits into the index\\nscatters colliding branches without enlarging the array or adding any\\nnew state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the proven 32-entry 2-bit saturating-counter table unchanged, but\\nchange both the read index and the write-back index from pc[6:2] to\\npc[6:2] ^ pc[11:7]. In the IF stage this mixes bits 7-11 of the PC\\ninto the existing 5-bit index, reducing the chance that two branches\\nwhose lower five bits collide map to the same counter. In the EX stage\\nthe update index uses in.pc[6:2] ^ in.pc[11:7] so the counter write\\nhits exactly the entry that was read during fetch. The combinational\\ncost is a single 5-bit XOR, which Yosys will absorb into the existing\\nLUT fabric with negligible Fmax impact. No new flops, pipeline bundles,\\nor cont...\n  Line 43: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Add single global loop-exit predictor to eliminate BHT loop-exit mispredictions\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The 32-entry 2-bit BHT saturates to strongly-taken for backward branches,\\nso it mispredicts the final iteration of every inner loop. CoreMark is\\nloop-heavy (list traversal, matrix rows, CRC) and these exit mispredictions\\nare the last systematic control bubble. A prior 8-entry tagged loop\\npredictor regressed because its tag-CAM and per-entry state machines\\nhurt Fmax. A single global loop counter needs only ~45 flops and one\\n32-bit PC comparator, giving loop-exit prediction with negligible\\ncombinational overhead.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 32-entry 2-bit BHT saturates to strongly-taken for backward branches,\\nso it mispredicts the final iteration of every inner loop. CoreMark is\\nloop-heavy (list traversal, matrix rows, CRC) and these exit mispredictions\\nare the last systematic control bubble. A prior 8-entry tagged loop\\npredictor regressed because its tag-CAM and per-entry state machines\\nhurt Fmax. A single global loop counter needs only ~45 flops and one\\n32-bit PC comparator, giving loop-exit prediction with negligible\\ncombinational overhead.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single global loop-exit predictor to the IF stage. It keeps one\\nloop_pc (32-bit), one loop_len (8-bit), one loop_cnt (8-bit), and a\\nvalid bit. When the fetched PC matches loop_pc and the branch is backward,\\nthe predictor overrides the BHT: loop_cnt>0 predicts taken (and decrements),\\nloop_cnt==0 predicts not-taken. In EX, training is automatic: on every\\nbackward branch that matches loop_pc, increment a training counter while\\ntaken; on the first not-taken exit capture the count as loop_len and\\nreload loop_cnt. A new backward-branch mispredict in EX clears valid so\\nthe predictor retrains safely. For all other branches the existing BHT\\nand JAL logic remain unchanged. This removes the 2-cycle bubble on every\\ncorre...\n  Line 46: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r15s2\", \"title\": \"Exclude backward branches from BHT to eliminate aliasing pollution on forward branches\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current 32-entry 2-bit BHT is indexed by PC[6:2] and updated for every\\nresolved conditional branch. Backward branches (loops) are almost always\\ntaken and saturate their entries to 2'b11 almost immediately. When a\\nforward branch happens to alias with a hot backward branch, the backward\\nbranch\\u2019s constant \\\"taken\\\" updates keep overwriting the forward branch\\u2019s\\nstate, preventing the forward branch from ever training to its true bias.\\nThis destructive aliasing is the largest remaining accuracy loss in the\\n32-entry table, yet it can be fixed with zero extra hardware: backward\\nbranches can be predicted statically taken (they already are) and simply\\nomitted from the BHT update path, freeing their index slots exclusively\\nfor forward branches.\\n\\nPrior attempts to reduce aliasing (128-entry BHT, gshare, hash-mixing)\\nall failed because they enlarged combinational paths or added flop state.\\nThis approach removes updates, adds no state, and shortens no paths \\u2014 it\\nonly improves the *quality* of the information stored in the existing 32\\nentries.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current 32-entry 2-bit BHT is indexed by PC[6:2] and updated for every\\nresolved conditional branch. Backward branches (loops) are almost always\\ntaken and saturate their entries to 2'b11 almost immediately. When a\\nforward branch happens to alias with a hot backward branch, the backward\\nbranch\\u2019s constant \\\"taken\\\" updates keep overwriting the forward branch\\u2019s\\nstate, preventing the forward branch from ever training to its true bias.\\nThis destructive aliasing is the largest remaining accuracy loss in the\\n32-entry table, yet it can be fixed with zero extra hardware: backward\\nbranches can be predicted statically taken (they already are) and simply\\nomitted from the BHT update path, freeing...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep3/log.jsonl:\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Add BTFNT fetch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and only redirects when a branch or jump reaches EX, so every taken CoreMark loop branch pays a multi-cycle wrong-path bubble. CoreMark is loop-heavy, and the present bench history has no prior predictor attempts, making branch penalty reduction the cleanest predictor-side opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core always fetches sequentially and only redirects when a branch or jump reaches EX, so every taken CoreMark loop branch pays a multi-cycle wrong-path bubble. CoreMark is loop-heavy, and the present bench history has no prior predictor attempts, making branch penalty reduction the cleanest predictor-side opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small static fetch predictor that predicts JAL and backward conditional branches taken, predicts forward conditional branches not taken, and carries the predicted next PC with the instruction into EX. EX should redirect only when the resolved next PC differs from the predicted next PC, eliminating the normal taken-backward-loop penalty while preserving exact RVFI pc_wdata, trap behavior, and the nret=1 port contract. This should improve CoreMark cycles with modest LUT/critical-path risk because it avoids tables and uses only IF-side opcode/offset decode plus one predicted-PC field in the pipeline bundles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted next-PC metadata to the IF/ID, ID/EX, and EX/MEM pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode fetched branch and JAL immediates in IF and select a BTFNT/JAL predicted next PC instead of always pc+4.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the predicted next PC from fetch into the ID/EX register across stalls and flushes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_st...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Add tiny EX-updated BTB\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core predicts every control-flow instruction as not taken and only redirects in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles repeatedly. The prior BTFNT predictor tried to remove those bubbles but added opcode decode, immediate extraction, and target adders to the IF/PC path, which likely consumed the cycle savings as Fmax loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core predicts every control-flow instruction as not taken and only redirects in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles repeatedly. The prior BTFNT predictor tried to remove those bubbles but added opcode decode, immediate extraction, and target adders to the IF/PC path, which likely consumed the cycle savings as Fmax loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer that is read in IF and updated only from resolved control-flow instructions in EX. Each entry stores valid, a compact PC tag, a resolved target, and a 2-bit taken counter; IF predicts taken only on tag hit with a taken counter and otherwise preserves the current sequential fetch behavior. This targets the same CoreMark loop-branch penalty as BTFNT while avoiding IF-side instruction decode and target addition, replacing them with a narrow tag compare and stored-target mux on the PC path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and prediction-hit/taken metadata to the pipeline bundles so EX can distinguish correct predictions from mispredictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Read a tiny direct-mapped BTB using the current PC and select the stored target only on a valid tag hit with a taken counter.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved next PC against the carried prediction, re...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Pipeline lookahead BTB\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The core still fetches sequentially until control flow resolves in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles. Prior predictor attempts showed that the main risk is not correctness but Fmax: IF-side BTFNT decode/target adders and same-cycle BTB lookup logic lengthened the PC feedback path enough to erase cycle savings. The remaining promising predictor angle is to keep dynamic branch learning while moving lookup work off the PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core still fetches sequentially until control flow resolves in EX, so hot CoreMark loop branches and direct jumps pay wrong-path bubbles. Prior predictor attempts showed that the main risk is not correctness but Fmax: IF-side BTFNT decode/target adders and same-cycle BTB lookup logic lengthened the PC feedback path enough to erase cycle savings. The remaining promising predictor angle is to keep dynamic branch learning while moving lookup work off the PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped lookahead BTB whose tag/counter/target lookup is performed for the next sequential PC and registered one cycle before that PC reaches IF. The PC update path should then choose between EX redirect, the already-registered prediction for the current PC, and pc+4; no same-cycle table read, tag compare, counter check, or target read feeds the PC register. EX updates the table on resolved conditional branches and direct JALs, carries the predicted next PC with each instruction, and redirects only when the resolved next PC differs from the carried prediction. This should recover repeated taken-loop and direct-jump bubbles while avoiding the Fmax loss seen in the prior IF-side predictors.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and prediction-val...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Add one-entry loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The core still pays an EX-stage redirect bubble on every taken loop branch and direct jump.\\nPrior predictor attempts lost fitness because IF-side instruction decode, target adders,\\nBTB arrays, tag/counter lookup, or broad prediction metadata increased front-end timing\\ncost more than they saved in CoreMark cycles. A narrower predictor should target only the\\nhottest repeated taken control-flow PCs while keeping the PC path close to the current\\nsequential fetch design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core still pays an EX-stage redirect bubble on every taken loop branch and direct jump.\\nPrior predictor attempts lost fitness because IF-side instruction decode, target adders,\\nBTB arrays, tag/counter lookup, or broad prediction metadata increased front-end timing\\ncost more than they saved in CoreMark cycles. A narrower predictor should target only the\\nhottest repeated taken control-flow PCs while keeping the PC path close to the current\\nsequential fetch design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry last-taken target predictor for conditional branches and direct JALs.\\nThe entry stores one full PC tag, one resolved target, and a tiny 2-bit confidence counter;\\nIF predicts taken only on an exact tag hit with the counter MSB set, otherwise it fetches\\npc+4 exactly as today. EX carries and compares the predicted next PC against the resolved\\nnext PC, redirecting only on mismatch, so correct hot-loop predictions avoid the normal\\ntaken-branch bubble while loop exits are repaired precisely. This avoids IF opcode decode,\\nimmediate generation, multi-entry BTB muxing, and lookahead-table plumbing, trading one\\ncomparator and one target mux for fewer CoreMark loop-control cycles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC validity and value from IF ...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage JAL predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempts all attacked conditional loop branches from the\\nfetch side and regressed because IF-side decode, BTB lookup, target muxing, or\\nbroad prediction metadata lengthened the front-end timing path more than they\\nrecovered in CoreMark cycles. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target\\nonly an always-taken control-flow class with much narrower hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempts all attacked conditional loop branches from the\\nfetch side and regressed because IF-side decode, BTB lookup, target muxing, or\\nbroad prediction metadata lengthened the front-end timing path more than they\\nrecovered in CoreMark cycles. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target\\nonly an always-taken control-flow class with much narrower hardware.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict direct JAL instructions as taken in decode, using the decoder and\\nimmediate generator that already exist in the ID stage, and redirect the PC one\\ncycle earlier than the current EX-stage jump resolution. Carry a minimal\\npredicted-next-PC bit/value with the JAL into EX so EX suppresses the duplicate\\nredirect when the predicted target matches, but still repairs any mismatch and\\nleaves JALR and conditional branches on the current conservative path. Gate the\\nearly redirect on valid, unstalled, aligned direct JALs so misaligned jump traps,\\nRVFI pc_wdata, and the nret=1 contract remain unchanged. This should save a\\nwrong-path bubble on calls and unconditional loop/control jumps without adding\\ntables, counters, branch comparators, or IF-side opcode/branch-immediate logic.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add minimal predicted-next-PC metadata to the ID/EX payload for decode-predicted direct JALs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": ...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"ID backward branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays the EX-stage redirect penalty on hot taken loop branches, but every prior fetch-side predictor in this core lost more Fmax than it recovered in CPI. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target only high-frequency backward conditional branches while avoiding BTB arrays, counters, direct-JAL handling, and full predicted-PC metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays the EX-stage redirect penalty on hot taken loop branches, but every prior fetch-side predictor in this core lost more Fmax than it recovered in CPI. The remaining predictor opportunity is to target only high-frequency backward conditional branches while avoiding BTB arrays, counters, direct-JAL handling, and full predicted-PC metadata.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a narrow decode-stage static predictor for valid backward conditional branches only. When ID sees a backward branch, redirect fetch to pc+imm one cycle before the current EX resolution point and carry only a single predicted-taken bit in ID/EX; EX recomputes the exact branch result and either suppresses the duplicate redirect on a correct taken prediction or repairs loop exits by redirecting to pc+4. This should recover one bubble on CoreMark loop backedges with less control/routing cost than the failed BTFNT, BTB, loop-entry, and direct-JAL predictors because there is no table lookup and no 32-bit predicted-next-PC field carried through the pipe.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit predicted-taken flag to the ID/EX payload, avoiding full predicted-PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize valid backward conditional branches, compute the early pc+imm target with the existing immediate path, and emit a predicted redirect plus the one-bit ID/EX marker.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/...\n  Line 22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every prior conditional-branch, BTB, loop, and direct-JAL predictor for this\\ncore has regressed because it added IF-side target lookup, branch immediate\\naddition, broad prediction metadata, or repair muxing that cost more Fmax than\\nCoreMark recovered in branch bubbles. The remaining predictor class not yet\\ntried is return prediction: CoreMark still executes function calls and returns,\\nand canonical JALR returns are always-taken control-flow instructions whose\\ntarget can come from a tiny return-address stack instead of an IF-side table or\\ntarget adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every prior conditional-branch, BTB, loop, and direct-JAL predictor for this\\ncore has regressed because it added IF-side target lookup, branch immediate\\naddition, broad prediction metadata, or repair muxing that cost more Fmax than\\nCoreMark recovered in branch bubbles. The remaining predictor class not yet\\ntried is return prediction: CoreMark still executes function calls and returns,\\nand canonical JALR returns are always-taken control-flow instructions whose\\ntarget can come from a tiny return-address stack instead of an IF-side table or\\ntarget adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry return-address stack updated by resolved call and return\\ninstructions in EX, and consult it only in ID for canonical returns such as\\nJALR x0, 0(x1). When ID sees a valid unstalled return and the stack is\\nnon-empty, redirect the PC for the next fetch to the predicted return address\\nwhile allowing the return instruction itself to advance normally. EX carries the\\npredicted target, suppresses the normal JALR redirect only when the resolved\\ntarget matches, and repairs mismatches exactly like the current EX redirect.\\nThis targets always-taken return bubbles with much less front-end timing risk\\nthan BTFNT, BTB, loop, or direct-JAL prediction because the predicted target is\\na smal...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"EX-local return target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they put opcode decode,\\ntarget adders, BTB/RAS lookup, or prediction-repair metadata on the front-end PC\\npath. The closest miss was the return predictor, which suggests return targets are\\nrelevant but the ID-to-IF early redirect machinery was too expensive. A predictor\\nthat stays local to EX can target Fmax by removing canonical-return JALR target\\ncalculation from the same-cycle redirect path without adding another fetch-stage\\npredictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts for this core regressed because they put opcode decode,\\ntarget adders, BTB/RAS lookup, or prediction-repair metadata on the front-end PC\\npath. The closest miss was the return predictor, which suggests return targets are\\nrelevant but the ID-to-IF early redirect machinery was too expensive. A predictor\\nthat stays local to EX can target Fmax by removing canonical-return JALR target\\ncalculation from the same-cycle redirect path without adding another fetch-stage\\npredictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny EX-local return target predictor for canonical returns. Track two\\nreturn addresses from resolved JAL/JALR call instructions in EX, and when EX sees a\\nvalid canonical return such as JALR x0, 0(x1), drive the existing redirect target\\nfrom the predicted return address rather than from the forwarded rs1+imm adder.\\nCompute the architectural JALR target in parallel only for RVFI pc_wdata, trap\\ndetection, and a registered next-cycle repair if the prediction was wrong or the\\ntarget was misaligned. Route non-return JALR instructions through the same delayed\\nredirect path so the forwarded rs1+imm adder no longer feeds the same-cycle PC\\nupdate mux; leave conditional branches and direct JALs unchanged. This avoids the\\nfailed IF/ID predictor pattern while potentially improving Fmax on the EX-to-IF\\nred...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Shadowed hot-taken predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still loses cycles on repeated taken loop branches and direct jumps,\\nbut every prior predictor that added IF/ID target arithmetic, BTB lookup\\nmetadata, or return-stack repair logic has regressed. The remaining predictor\\nopportunity is to keep the front-end prediction state extremely narrow and\\navoid carrying a full predicted next PC through the existing pipeline bundles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still loses cycles on repeated taken loop branches and direct jumps,\\nbut every prior predictor that added IF/ID target arithmetic, BTB lookup\\nmetadata, or return-stack repair logic has regressed. The remaining predictor\\nopportunity is to keep the front-end prediction state extremely narrow and\\navoid carrying a full predicted next PC through the existing pipeline bundles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry hot-taken predictor for static branch/JAL instructions that\\nstores the resolved PC, full instruction word, and resolved aligned target\\nafter EX observes a taken static control-flow instruction. IF predicts taken\\nonly when both the current PC and fetched instruction exactly match the entry,\\nso it needs no opcode decode, immediate generation, or target adder on the\\nfetch path. Carry only a one-bit predicted-taken marker into EX; because the\\nentry is keyed by the exact instruction word, EX can recompute the same static\\ntarget and suppress the normal redirect on correct predictions, or repair loop\\nexits by redirecting to pc+4. Clear or weaken the entry on a matching not-taken\\nconditional branch. This targets the hottest repeated taken PCs while avoiding\\nthe full predicted-next-PC fields and broad repair plumbing that hurt earlier\\npredictor attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single predicted-taken bit to the IF/ID and...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Add redirect target replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that conventional branch predictors have regressed\\nbecause they put decode, target adders, BTB/RAS lookup, instruction matching,\\nor full prediction repair metadata on timing-sensitive front-end paths. The\\nremaining predictor opportunity is to recover some taken-control-flow cost\\nwithout predicting the next PC before EX resolves the branch.\\n\\nCoreMark is loop-heavy and the apples-to-apples cosim injects randomized\\ninstruction-memory stalls. A hot taken branch repeatedly fetches the same\\nredirect target, so remembering the target instruction can avoid target-fetch\\niStall bubbles while leaving the existing not-taken front end and EX redirect\\nsemantics intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench history shows that conventional branch predictors have regressed\\nbecause they put decode, target adders, BTB/RAS lookup, instruction matching,\\nor full prediction repair metadata on timing-sensitive front-end paths. The\\nremaining predictor opportunity is to recover some taken-control-flow cost\\nwithout predicting the next PC before EX resolves the branch.\\n\\nCoreMark is loop-heavy and the apples-to-apples cosim injects randomized\\ninstruction-memory stalls. A hot taken branch repeatedly fetches the same\\nredirect target, so remembering the target instruction can avoid target-fetch\\niStall bubbles while leaving the existing not-taken front end and EX redirect\\nsemantics intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry redirect-target replay predictor in IF. When a taken branch\\nor jump redirects to a target and the target instruction is later fetched\\nsuccessfully, remember {target_pc, target_instr}. On later fetches of the\\nsame PC, especially immediately after another EX redirect, allow IF to supply\\nthe remembered instruction and treat the fetch as internally ready even if\\nio_imemReady is low. The external imem address ...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Direct-JAL target memo predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench-core predictor history shows that conditional branch prediction,\\nBTBs, RAS schemes, exact-instruction matching, and target replay have all\\ntended to lose more Fmax than they recover in CoreMark CPI. The remaining\\nuseful predictor target is direct JAL: it is always taken, has a stable\\nresolved target after first execution, and needs no branch-condition compare,\\ncounter update policy, return stack, or instruction-side immediate decode in\\nthe fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench-core predictor history shows that conditional branch prediction,\\nBTBs, RAS schemes, exact-instruction matching, and target replay have all\\ntended to lose more Fmax than they recover in CoreMark CPI. The remaining\\nuseful predictor target is direct JAL: it is always taken, has a stable\\nresolved target after first execution, and needs no branch-condition compare,\\ncounter update policy, return stack, or instruction-side immediate decode in\\nthe fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry direct-JAL target memo. EX updates the entry only when a\\nvalid, aligned, non-JALR JAL retires through the existing resolved-target\\npath. IF predicts only on a full-PC tag hit, selecting the stored target\\ninstead of pc+4, and carries a predicted-next-PC value with that instruction\\nso EX can suppress the duplicate redirect when correct or repair any stale\\nsymbolic-stream mismatch precisely. This should save repeated direct-call and\\nunconditional-jump bubbles in CoreMark while being narrower than prior\\npredictor attempts: no conditional branch table, no counters, no RAS, no\\nfetched-instruction match feeding the PC mux, and no target adder in IF.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add minimal predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX for JAL-memo hits only.\"},...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Predict imem stall replay hits\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors for this core repeatedly regressed because they put\\ntarget lookup, target arithmetic, instruction matching, or wide repair\\nmetadata on the PC redirect path. CoreMark is still evaluated with randomized\\ninstruction-memory stalls, and hot loop bodies repeatedly refetch the same\\nsmall set of instruction PCs. A predictor that only speculates on the fetched\\ninstruction word during an imem stall can target CPI without predicting a new\\nPC or changing control-flow resolution.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors for this core repeatedly regressed because they put\\ntarget lookup, target arithmetic, instruction matching, or wide repair\\nmetadata on the PC redirect path. CoreMark is still evaluated with randomized\\ninstruction-memory stalls, and hot loop bodies repeatedly refetch the same\\nsmall set of instruction PCs. A predictor that only speculates on the fetched\\ninstruction word during an imem stall can target CPI without predicting a new\\nPC or changing control-flow resolution.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped fetch replay predictor inside IF that remembers a few\\nsuccessfully fetched {pc, instr} pairs. On a later cycle where io_imemReady is\\nlow, IF may supply the cached instruction as valid only when the current PC tag\\nhits; otherwise it preserves today's NOP/bubble behavior. The PC still advances\\nsequentially or redirects exactly as it does now, and EX remains the sole\\nbranch/jump resolver, so this avoids the failed BTB/BTFNT/RAS pattern of\\nfeeding predicted targets into the PC mux. Under the 22 percent randomized\\niStall model, repeated CoreMark loop instructions should occasionally decode\\nthrough an otherwise lost imem-stall cycle, while misses and redirects fall\\nback to the existing conservative path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"f...\n  Line 40: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"EX-local branch direction predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still executes many conditional branches, but prior predictor attempts\\nregressed whenever prediction added IF-side target lookup, instruction decode,\\ntarget adders, replay muxes, return-stack repair, or wide predicted-PC metadata.\\nA predictor that stays entirely in EX can target the branch redirect timing cone\\nwithout perturbing the sensitive fetch/decode path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still executes many conditional branches, but prior predictor attempts\\nregressed whenever prediction added IF-side target lookup, instruction decode,\\ntarget adders, replay muxes, return-stack repair, or wide predicted-PC metadata.\\nA predictor that stays entirely in EX can target the branch redirect timing cone\\nwithout perturbing the sensitive fetch/decode path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny EX-local conditional-branch direction predictor, such as a 4-entry\\n2-bit saturating BHT indexed by the branch PC. For aligned conditional branches,\\nuse the predicted direction rather than the freshly computed rs1/rs2 comparison\\nto drive the same-cycle redirect enable; keep the existing branch-target adder\\nand keep direct JAL/JALR behavior unchanged. Compute the real branch condition\\nin parallel for RVFI pc_wdata, trap/misalignment handling, BHT update, and a\\none-cycle precise repair redirect when prediction disagrees with reality.\\n\\nCorrectly predicted taken branches keep the current one-cycle EX redirect cost,\\ncorrectly predicted not-taken branches keep the current fall-through behavior,\\nand only mispredictions pay an extra repair bubble. The intended win is Fmax:\\nthe high-fanout forwarded-operand branch comparator no longer feeds the\\nsame-cycle PC redirect control on the hot path, while avoiding all front-end\\npredictor machinery that has previously regressed this core.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"...\n  Line 43: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Static EX branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly lost by putting target lookup,\\ninstruction decode, replay muxes, return stacks, or wide predicted-PC metadata\\non the already-sensitive fetch/decode path. The one EX-local direction\\npredictor also regressed, but it still paid for BHT state, update policy, and\\nrepair plumbing. A narrower predictor angle is to keep speculation entirely in\\nEX and make the common backward-loop redirect independent of the forwarded\\noperand branch comparator.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly lost by putting target lookup,\\ninstruction decode, replay muxes, return stacks, or wide predicted-PC metadata\\non the already-sensitive fetch/decode path. The one EX-local direction\\npredictor also regressed, but it still paid for BHT state, update policy, and\\nrepair plumbing. A narrower predictor angle is to keep speculation entirely in\\nEX and make the common backward-loop redirect independent of the forwarded\\noperand branch comparator.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the same-cycle redirect decision for conditional branches with a\\ntable-free static direction prediction in EX: backward branches predict taken,\\nforward branches predict not taken. Compute the real branch condition in\\nparallel for RVFI pc_wdata and precise repair; on a predicted-taken loop exit,\\nissue a one-cycle repair redirect to pc+4, while forward taken branches can\\nstill use the existing resolved redirect path. This should avoid all failed\\nfront-end predictor patterns and remove the high-fanout branch comparator from\\nthe hot backward-loop redirect enable, at the cost of rare loop-exit repair\\nbubbles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add table-free EX-local backward-taken/forward-not-taken branch prediction, keep actual branch co...\n  Line 46: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r15s2\", \"title\": \"Sideband one-entry loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\ninstruction decode, target arithmetic, BTB/RAS lookup, replay muxing, or wide\\npredicted-PC metadata to timing-sensitive fetch/decode paths. CoreMark still\\nhas hot backward loop branches, but the predictor must be narrower than the\\nearlier BTFNT/BTB/RAS variants to have a chance of improving the fitness\\nproduct.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts for this core mostly regressed because they added\\ninstruction decode, target arithmetic, BTB/RAS lookup, replay muxing, or wide\\npredicted-PC metadata to timing-sensitive fetch/decode paths. CoreMark still\\nhas hot backward loop branches, but the predictor must be narrower than the\\nearlier BTFNT/BTB/RAS variants to have a chance of improving the fitness\\nproduct.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry backward-conditional-branch target predictor that stores a\\nfull PC tag, resolved target, and a small confidence bit only after EX resolves\\na valid taken backward branch. IF predicts taken only on an exact tag hit and\\nselects the stored target instead of pc+4; otherwise the front end behaves\\nexactly as today. Instead of widening the packed IF/ID/ID/EX structs with a\\nfull predicted-next-PC field, carry the prediction hit and target through a\\nsmall sideband register in core.sv that advances, stalls, and flushes with the\\nID/EX register.\\n\\nEX then suppresses the duplicate redirect when a predicted branch really is\\ntaken to the stored target, repairs loop exits by redirecting to pc+4, and\\nleaves non-predicted branches, JAL, and JALR on the current resolved path. The\\nintended win is one fewer bubble on repeated CoreMark loop backedges while\\navoiding the front-end opcode decode, target adder, multi-entry table muxing,\\nand packed-pipeline metadata that hurt earlier predictor hypotheses.\\n\", \"expected_imp...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep2/log.jsonl:\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static IF backward branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and redirects on every taken control transfer. CoreMark spends much of its timed region in tight loops, so backward loop branches repeatedly pay a wrong-path bubble even though their target is statically knowable from the fetched instruction. A small static predictor can recover those bubbles while preserving the single-issue RVFI contract and avoiding a large Fmax hit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and redirects on every taken control transfer. CoreMark spends much of its timed region in tight loops, so backward loop branches repeatedly pay a wrong-path bubble even though their target is statically knowable from the fetched instruction. A small static predictor can recover those bubbles while preserving the single-issue RVFI contract and avoiding a large Fmax hit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add IF-stage predecode for conditional branches and JAL. Predict JAL as taken, predict conditional branches as taken only when the B-immediate is negative, and otherwise predict fall-through. Carry the predicted next PC through ID/EX, then change EX redirect generation to redirect only when the resolved next PC differs from the prediction. This should remove most loop-back branch bubbles in CoreMark with only modest mux/add/sign-extension logic in the fetch path and no stateful predictor tables.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles so EX can identify prediction mismatches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predecode fetched JAL and backward conditional branch immediates, choose the predicted next PC, and emit prediction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"descr...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"PC-indexed BTB predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous static predictor recovered some loop-control bubbles but put\\ninstruction predecode and immediate generation on the fetch next-PC path,\\nwhich is risky for Tang Nano 20K Fmax. CoreMark still has repeated taken\\nloop branches and some direct/indirect jumps, so a predictor can help if the\\nlookup path is driven only by the registered fetch PC rather than by\\nio_imemData.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous static predictor recovered some loop-control bubbles but put\\ninstruction predecode and immediate generation on the fetch next-PC path,\\nwhich is risky for Tang Nano 20K Fmax. CoreMark still has repeated taken\\nloop branches and some direct/indirect jumps, so a predictor can help if the\\nlookup path is driven only by the registered fetch PC rather than by\\nio_imemData.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped PC-indexed BTB with a 2-bit taken counter and target\\nper entry. IF predicts taken only on a tag hit with a taken counter and uses\\nthe stored target; otherwise it fetches pc+4. EX resolves the actual next PC,\\nupdates the table for branches and jumps, and redirects only when the resolved\\nnext PC differs from the carried prediction.\\n\\nThis should recover most steady-state CoreMark loop-back bubbles after warmup\\nwithout the failed imem_data predecode critical path. The cost is a small\\nPC-indexed LUT/register table and a compare/mux in IF, so the expected gain is\\nmodest but more Fmax-safe than static fetch predecode.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted next-PC metadata from IF through ID/EX so EX can detect prediction mismatches without changing the NRET=1 RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small PC-indexed BTB/BHT lookup and update interface, selecting either the stored predicted tar...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"One-entry hot redirect predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempts show that CoreMark has exploitable taken loop\\ncontrol flow, but larger or instruction-predecode-based predictors hurt the\\nTang Nano 20K fetch critical path enough to erase the cycle savings. The most\\npromising remaining predictor angle is to keep the frontend prediction logic\\ndeliberately tiny and only target the hottest repeated redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous predictor attempts show that CoreMark has exploitable taken loop\\ncontrol flow, but larger or instruction-predecode-based predictors hurt the\\nTang Nano 20K fetch critical path enough to erase the cycle savings. The most\\npromising remaining predictor angle is to keep the frontend prediction logic\\ndeliberately tiny and only target the hottest repeated redirect.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry tagged redirect cache in IF. On an exact fetch-PC tag hit,\\npredict the cached target as the next PC; otherwise fetch pc+4. EX carries the\\npredicted-next metadata, resolves the actual next PC for every retiring\\ninstruction, redirects only on a prediction mismatch, updates the entry on\\ntaken branches and jumps, and invalidates it when the same PC resolves not\\ntaken or non-control.\\n\\nThis recovers steady-state bubbles for one dominant CoreMark loop backedge or\\nrepeated jump while avoiding the failed imem_data predecode path and avoiding\\na multi-entry BTB/BHT table. The hardware cost should be one valid bit, one\\nPC tag compare, one target register, and a next-PC mux, so the Fmax risk is\\nmuch lower than the prior predictor designs.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and predicted-taken metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so EX can validate the one-entry prediction precisely.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\":...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Return-address stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts on this core regressed because they put too much\\ntarget-generation or tag-lookup logic on the shallow fetch critical path.\\nCoreMark still contains repeated call/return control flow in addition to\\nloops, and returns are the one dynamic-control case where a tiny predictor\\ncan provide a target without a BTB target array or branch-immediate adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts on this core regressed because they put too much\\ntarget-generation or tag-lookup logic on the shallow fetch critical path.\\nCoreMark still contains repeated call/return control flow in addition to\\nloops, and returns are the one dynamic-control case where a tiny predictor\\ncan provide a target without a BTB target array or branch-immediate adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small return-address stack for JALR return prediction only. Push\\npc+4 when a legal link-register JAL/JALR call resolves, pop when a legal\\nreturn resolves, and in IF predict a fetched `jalr x0, x1/x5, 0` to the\\ncurrent stack top. Carry predicted-next-PC metadata to EX so a correctly\\npredicted return does not flush, while any mismatch redirects and repairs\\nthe stack precisely.\\n\\nThis intentionally does not predict conditional branches and does not add\\na PC-indexed BTB/tag compare. The IF critical-path addition should be only\\na narrow opcode/rd/rs1/imm-zero return recognizer plus a mux to the stack\\ntop, so Fmax risk is lower than the failed static branch, BTB, and hot\\nredirect predictor variants. The cycle gain is expected to be modest but\\npositive if CoreMark's timed call/return traffic is frequent enough.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and return-predicted metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for precise EX validation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Low-bit loop backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts on this core regressed because full IF predecode,\\nBTB tag/target lookup, hot redirect caching, and return prediction all added\\ntoo much logic to the shallow fetch next-PC path. CoreMark still has repeated\\ncounted-loop backedges, but the predictor must be narrower than those failed\\ndesigns to have any chance of improving fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts on this core regressed because full IF predecode,\\nBTB tag/target lookup, hot redirect caching, and return prediction all added\\ntoo much logic to the shallow fetch next-PC path. CoreMark still has repeated\\ncounted-loop backedges, but the predictor must be narrower than those failed\\ndesigns to have any chance of improving fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry conditional-loop backedge predictor that learns only a hot\\ntaken backward conditional branch at EX and predicts it in IF using a small\\nlow-bit PC match, for example pc[7:2], plus a stored target. Do not predict\\nJAL, JALR, forward branches, or returns, and do not keep a full tag, table,\\nor saturating counters. Carry predicted next-PC metadata with the instruction\\nso EX redirects only on mismatch and updates or clears the entry from the\\nresolved branch.\\n\\nThis intentionally accepts occasional low-bit alias mispredictions because\\nthey are corrected precisely at EX, while making the IF critical-path addition\\nmuch smaller than the previous predictor designs. If CoreMark has one dominant\\ninner-loop backedge, the cycle savings may outweigh the small compare and mux;\\nif aliasing or routing dominates, the hypothesis should fail cleanly without\\nweakening RVFI or trap invariants.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted next-PC and predicted-valid fields to IF/ID and ID/EX bund...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Countdown loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts recovered plausible branch bubbles but regressed because\\nthey put instruction predecode, PC tag compares, target-table lookup, or return\\ndecode directly on the shallow fetch next-PC path. CoreMark still has repeated\\ncounted-loop backedges, so the remaining promising predictor angle is one that\\navoids both imem_data decode and PC-indexed table lookup during fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts recovered plausible branch bubbles but regressed because\\nthey put instruction predecode, PC tag compares, target-table lookup, or return\\ndecode directly on the shallow fetch next-PC path. CoreMark still has repeated\\ncounted-loop backedges, so the remaining promising predictor angle is one that\\navoids both imem_data decode and PC-indexed table lookup during fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry countdown loop predictor trained only by resolved taken\\nbackward conditional branches. When EX sees a taken backedge, record its target\\nand the small instruction-distance from target to branch, then arm a fetch-side\\ndown-counter after redirecting to the loop target. IF decrements the counter\\nonly on real fetch advances; when it expires, it predicts the next PC as the\\nrecorded target without doing any PC tag compare or instruction predecode.\\n\\nCarry predicted-next metadata with each fetched instruction so EX can validate\\nevery prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect on mismatch, and retrain\\nor disable the countdown entry cleanly. This intentionally targets one stable\\ninner-loop backedge and accepts occasional precise mispredictions, trading a\\ntiny counter-zero test and target mux for avoiding the failed predictor logic\\nthat previously lengthened the IF critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add...\n  Line 22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Decode-stage direct JAL predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts on this core regressed because they added BTB, RAS,\\nbranch-predecode, or countdown repair logic to the shallow fetch/control path.\\nThe remaining low-risk control-flow opportunity is direct JAL: its target is\\nknown from the fetched instruction and PC, it is always taken when legal, and\\nit does not require a branch comparator, history table, or return stack.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts on this core regressed because they added BTB, RAS,\\nbranch-predecode, or countdown repair logic to the shallow fetch/control path.\\nThe remaining low-risk control-flow opportunity is direct JAL: its target is\\nknown from the fetched instruction and PC, it is always taken when legal, and\\nit does not require a branch comparator, history table, or return stack.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict only legal aligned direct JAL instructions one stage earlier, in ID.\\nWhen ID is actually advancing and sees a non-JALR JAL with an aligned target,\\nupdate the fetch PC to the J-immediate target while still allowing the JAL\\nitself to enter ID/EX. Carry a one-bit early-JAL prediction marker into EX so\\nEX still produces the correct RVFI pc_wdata and link result but suppresses the\\nredundant redirect for that already-predicted JAL. Leave conditional branches,\\nJALR, misaligned JAL targets, and all dynamic predictor state on the existing\\nEX-resolution path.\\n\\nThis recovers one redirect bubble for direct calls and unconditional jumps in\\nCoreMark without a fetch-side table, PC tag compare, return decoder, or branch\\nimmediate predictor. The expected gain is modest but more Fmax-safe than the\\nprior predictor designs because the new PC feedback is JAL-only and can be\\ngated by ID advance rather than placed on every fetch next-PC decision.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rt...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Registered lookahead backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts found exploitable CoreMark loop control flow but lost\\nmore Fmax than they recovered because branch predecode, BTB lookup, tag\\ncompares, or return recognition landed directly on the shallow fetch next-PC\\npath. The remaining promising predictor angle is to keep the PC update path\\ndriven only by registered state while still learning one hot loop backedge.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts found exploitable CoreMark loop control flow but lost\\nmore Fmax than they recovered because branch predecode, BTB lookup, tag\\ncompares, or return recognition landed directly on the shallow fetch next-PC\\npath. The remaining promising predictor angle is to keep the PC update path\\ndriven only by registered state while still learning one hot loop backedge.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry conditional-backedge predictor with a one-cycle lookahead\\nlookup. When EX resolves a taken backward conditional branch, store its branch\\nPC, the preceding sequential PC, and the target. While fetching normally, IF\\ncompares the current PC against the stored preceding PC and registers whether\\nthe next sequential instruction is the learned branch; on the following cycle,\\nthe PC mux uses only that registered predict-valid bit and registered target.\\n\\nThis should recover steady-state bubbles for a dominant CoreMark loop branch\\nwithout putting a BTB table, current-PC tag compare, instruction predecode, or\\nbranch-immediate adder on the same-cycle fetch next-PC path. EX still carries\\npredicted-next metadata, redirects precisely on mismatch, and disables the\\nentry on loop exit or aliasing, preserving RVFI order, trap behavior, and the\\nNRET=1 top-level contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact predicted-next-PC meta...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Redirect target replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts found real CoreMark loop-control opportunity, but every design that changed the normal fetch next-PC path with predecode, PC compares, target muxes, or prediction metadata lost more Fmax than it recovered in CPI. The remaining predictor angle should avoid making every sequential fetch pay for loop prediction.\\n\\nA tiny target-instruction replay buffer can target the same repeated taken backedges without predicting the PC ahead of time. It only acts on an actual EX redirect and tries to fill the redirect bubble with a previously observed target instruction, so the common pc+4 fetch path stays essentially unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts found real CoreMark loop-control opportunity, but every design that changed the normal fetch next-PC path with predecode, PC compares, target muxes, or prediction metadata lost more Fmax than it recovered in CPI. The remaining predictor angle should avoid making every sequential fetch pay for loop prediction.\\n\\nA tiny target-instruction replay buffer can target the same repeated taken backedges without predicting the PC ahead of time. It only acts on an actual EX redirect and tries to fill the redirect bubble with a previously observed target instruction, so the common pc+4 fetch path stays essentially unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry redirect-target replay predictor inside IF. When a taken branch or jump redirects to a target and the target instruction is fetched normally, record the target PC and instruction word. On a later EX redirect to the same target, emit the cached target instruction and target PC into ID during the redirect cycle instead of forcing a NOP, while still updating the real PC to the redirect target.\\n\\nThis should recover one bubble for hot loop backedges after warmup without a BTB lookup, current-PC tag compare, imem_data ...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny ifetch replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors for this core consistently regressed because they put\\ntarget lookup, instruction predecode, prediction metadata, or repair muxing on\\nthe shallow fetch/redirect path. The apples-to-apples CoreMark mode also has\\nroughly 22% random instruction-bus stalls, and the current frontend turns every\\nlow io_imemReady cycle into a NOP even when the same hot-loop PC was fetched\\ncorrectly on an earlier iteration.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors for this core consistently regressed because they put\\ntarget lookup, instruction predecode, prediction metadata, or repair muxing on\\nthe shallow fetch/redirect path. The apples-to-apples CoreMark mode also has\\nroughly 22% random instruction-bus stalls, and the current frontend turns every\\nlow io_imemReady cycle into a NOP even when the same hot-loop PC was fetched\\ncorrectly on an earlier iteration.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped instruction replay predictor trained only by accepted\\ninstruction fetches. On an io_imemReady=1 fetch, record {PC tag, instruction}\\nin an 8-entry table. On a later io_imemReady=0 cycle, if the current PC hits\\nthe table, supply the cached instruction and treat fetch as effectively ready;\\notherwise keep the existing stall/NOP behavior.\\n\\nThis is not a branch target predictor and does not carry prediction metadata\\ninto EX. It attacks repeated hot-loop iStalls while avoiding the failed IF\\nbranch-predecode/BTB/RAS/backedge-repair paths. Because the instruction memory\\nis immutable in FPGA/cosim and formal ties io_imemReady high, a replay hit is\\narchitecturally the same instruction word previously observed for that PC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an 8-entry PC-tagged instruction replay predictor, fill it on accepted ready fetches,...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Wider ifetch replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The only successful predictor result so far was the tiny instruction-fetch\\nreplay table, because it converts random hot-loop instruction-bus stalls into\\nuseful decode cycles without adding branch target lookup, prediction repair,\\nor EX-stage metadata. CoreMark's timed working set is larger than eight\\nfetch-table slots, so direct-mapped conflicts can still turn repeated PCs\\nback into imem stalls even after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only successful predictor result so far was the tiny instruction-fetch\\nreplay table, because it converts random hot-loop instruction-bus stalls into\\nuseful decode cycles without adding branch target lookup, prediction repair,\\nor EX-stage metadata. CoreMark's timed working set is larger than eight\\nfetch-table slots, so direct-mapped conflicts can still turn repeated PCs\\nback into imem stalls even after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Expand the IF-stage instruction replay predictor from 8 direct-mapped entries\\nto 16 direct-mapped entries while keeping the exact same training and use\\ndiscipline: fill only on accepted io_imemReady fetches, replay only when\\nio_imemReady is low and the current PC tag hits, and leave all branch/jump\\nprediction behavior unchanged. This should reduce replay conflict misses in\\nCoreMark hot loops under the 22% random iStall model while avoiding the\\ntarget-PC muxing and prediction-repair metadata that made earlier branch\\npredictors regress. The added hardware is one more index bit and a modestly\\nlarger tag/instruction array, so the expected gain is smaller than the first\\nreplay predictor but still plausible.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Increase the PC-tagged instruction replay predictor to 16 direct-mapped entries, preserving fill-on-ready and replay-on-stall semantics.\"}...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Line-packed ifetch replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The only predictor that improved this bench core was the tiny instruction-fetch replay table, which converts random hot-loop iStall cycles into useful decoded instructions without branch target prediction or EX repair metadata. Follow-up attempts to widen the table or remove payload resets regressed, suggesting the remaining replay cost is tag/control routing rather than capacity or reset muxing.\\n\\nA predictor refinement should therefore keep the accepted-fetch-only replay semantics but reduce the fetch-stage compare and tag storage pressure that now sits on every stalled fetch decision.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only predictor that improved this bench core was the tiny instruction-fetch replay table, which converts random hot-loop iStall cycles into useful decoded instructions without branch target prediction or EX repair metadata. Follow-up attempts to widen the table or remove payload resets regressed, suggesting the remaining replay cost is tag/control routing rather than capacity or reset muxing.\\n\\nA predictor refinement should therefore keep the accepted-fetch-only replay semantics but reduce the fetch-stage compare and tag storage pressure that now sits on every stalled fetch decision.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Repack the current 8-entry single-word replay table into four two-word replay lines. Each line stores one full PC tag, two instruction words, and per-word valid bits; accepted fetches fill only the addressed half, clearing the sibling valid bit when a different tag replaces the line. On io_imemReady=0, a replay hit still requires a full tag match plus the selected half valid bit, then supplies the cached immutable instruction and asserts imem_effective_ready exactly as today.\\n\\nThis preserves the successful no-branch-prediction replay model and the NRET=1 RVFI contract while halving the number of stored tags and tag-select ro...\n  Line 40: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Redirect-filtered replay training\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The only predictor that has improved this core is the tiny accepted-fetch\\ninstruction replay table, because it hides random hot-loop iStalls without\\nadding branch target lookup or EX repair metadata. The current IF code still\\nfills the direct-mapped replay entry on any io_imemReady cycle, including the\\ncycle an EX redirect kills the fetched sequential wrong-path instruction.\\nIn tight taken loops, those wrong-path fall-through fills can repeatedly\\nevict useful loop-body replay entries with the same pc[4:2] index.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only predictor that has improved this core is the tiny accepted-fetch\\ninstruction replay table, because it hides random hot-loop iStalls without\\nadding branch target lookup or EX repair metadata. The current IF code still\\nfills the direct-mapped replay entry on any io_imemReady cycle, including the\\ncycle an EX redirect kills the fetched sequential wrong-path instruction.\\nIn tight taken loops, those wrong-path fall-through fills can repeatedly\\nevict useful loop-body replay entries with the same pc[4:2] index.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the existing 8-entry replay predictor lookup and hit semantics exactly\\nas they are, but train the table only when the fetched instruction is not\\nbeing killed by a redirect/flush. On an EX redirect, update the architectural\\nPC to the target as today but skip the replay fill for the wrong-path word at\\nthe old PC. If that fall-through path is later executed for real, it will be\\nfetched and trained on a non-redirect cycle.\\n\\nThis should improve replay hit quality for CoreMark's repeated taken loops\\nwithout adding a new IF tag compare, target mux, branch predecode path, or\\nprediction metadata. The change is intentionally small: it may recover a few\\niStall bubbles by reducing direct-mapped pollution while preserving Fmax and\\nall NRET=1 RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expec...\n  Line 43: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Folded-index ifetch replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The only predictor that has produced a large bench improvement is the accepted-fetch instruction replay table, because it hides random hot-loop iStalls without branch-target lookup, predecode, or EX repair metadata. Follow-up replay changes that widened the table, repacked lines, warmed during backend stalls, or filtered redirect fills all regressed, so the next predictor step should preserve the same simple lookup semantics and avoid adding new storage depth or control paths.\\n\\nThe current 8-entry table is indexed directly by pc[4:2], so instructions separated by 32-byte strides always evict each other. CoreMark loop bodies and call-heavy code can easily contain hot PCs with that spacing, causing replay misses even after each instruction has been fetched successfully before.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The only predictor that has produced a large bench improvement is the accepted-fetch instruction replay table, because it hides random hot-loop iStalls without branch-target lookup, predecode, or EX repair metadata. Follow-up replay changes that widened the table, repacked lines, warmed during backend stalls, or filtered redirect fills all regressed, so the next predictor step should preserve the same simple lookup semantics and avoid adding new storage depth or control paths.\\n\\nThe current 8-entry table is indexed directly by pc[4:2], so instructions separated by 32-byte strides always evict each other. CoreMark loop bodies and call-heavy code can easily contain hot PCs with that spacing, causing replay misses even after each instruction has been fetched successfully before.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the 8-entry accepted-fetch replay predictor exactly the same size and keep its fill-on-io_imemReady, replay-only-when-io_imemReady-is-low semantics, but replace the direct pc[4:2] index with a cheap folded hash such as pc[4:2] ^ pc[7:5]. Keep t...\n  Line 46: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r15s2\", \"title\": \"Victim-slot ifetch replay predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Branch-target predictors, RAS variants, countdown loop predictors, and\\nreplay-table capacity/hash changes have mostly regressed because extra PC\\nmuxing, EX repair metadata, or wider fetch routing cost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered in CoreMark cycles. The one predictor that improved this core is\\nthe accepted-fetch instruction replay table, which hides random hot-loop\\ninstruction stalls without predicting control flow.\\n\\nThe remaining plausible replay weakness is direct-mapped thrashing between a\\nsmall number of hot PCs that share pc[4:2]. A single victim slot targets that\\nspecific failure mode without widening the primary table or changing the\\nnormal pc+4 fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Branch-target predictors, RAS variants, countdown loop predictors, and\\nreplay-table capacity/hash changes have mostly regressed because extra PC\\nmuxing, EX repair metadata, or wider fetch routing cost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered in CoreMark cycles. The one predictor that improved this core is\\nthe accepted-fetch instruction replay table, which hides random hot-loop\\ninstruction stalls without predicting control flow.\\n\\nThe remaining plausible replay weakness is direct-mapped thrashing between a\\nsmall number of hot PCs that share pc[4:2]. A single victim slot targets that\\nspecific failure mode without widening the primary table or changing the\\nnormal pc+4 fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the current 8-entry accepted-fetch replay predictor as the primary\\nlookup, but add one fully-tagged victim replay slot. When an accepted fetch\\nreplaces a valid primary entry with a different tag, move the evicted tag and\\ninstruction into the victim slot. On io_imemReady=0, first check the indexed\\nprimary entry; if it misses, check the victim slot and replay its instruction\\non a tag hit. Do not predict branches or jumps, do not add predicted-nex...\n\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/bench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep1/log.jsonl:\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static backward-branch next-PC predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX, so every\\ntaken l