edictions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add combinational branch/jump prediction to the IF stage and suppress\\nEX-stage redirects when the prediction is correct.\\n\\n1. Extend if_id_t and id_ex_t with a single predict_taken bit.\\n2. In if_stage, instantiate a small imm_gen (or inline equivalent) to\\n   extract the B/J immediate from imem_data. Detect opcode 1100011\\n   (BRANCH) and 1101111 (JAL). Predict taken for JAL unconditionally,\\n   and for branches when the immediate sign bit is 1 (backward branch).\\n   On a predicted-taken instruction, redirect next_pc...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"32-entry direct-mapped BTB in IF stage to eliminate taken-branch bubbles\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches sequentially, paying a 2-cycle bubble for every\\ntaken branch and JAL. A prior static predictor that computed branch targets\\ncombinationally in IF from imem_data (decode -> imm_gen -> adder) slashed\\nFmax by ~40% because that path starts from the off-chip/BRAM instruction\\nword. A Branch Target Buffer (BTB) avoids that path entirely: it is indexed\\ndirectly by the PC register output, so the predicted next PC is just a small\\ndistributed-RAM read with no instruction decode or adder in the critical\\npath. On the Tang Nano 20K a 32-entry BTB costs only ~35 LUTs and reads in\\na single LUT delay, making it a much safer timing bet while still removing\\nthe branch bubble for loop-back edges once they warm up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches sequentially, paying a 2-cycle bubble for every\\ntaken branch and JAL. A prior static predictor that computed branch targets\\ncombinationally in IF from imem_data (decode -> imm_gen -> adder) slashed\\nFmax by ~40% because that path starts from the off-chip/BRAM instruction\\nword. A Branch Target Buffer (BTB) avoids that path entirely: it is indexed\\ndirectly by the PC register output, so the predicted next PC is just