{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1779280193547,"sessionID":"ses_1baa43bd5ffeTyBXYXMOzEdqBl","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"tool_read_Fb4wq1TBQlfMkUlCzEQ0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r1s0.yaml"},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gemini-3_5-flash-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r1s0.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260520-001-r1s0\n2: title: \"Precision load-use hazard detection and single 33-bit multiplier sharing\"\n3: category: micro_opt\n4: motivation: |\n5:   The current 5-stage pipeline has two major microarchitectural bottlenecks:\n6:   1. Spurious load-use stalls: The hazard unit stalls the pipeline whenever the rd of a load in EX matches the raw rs1/rs2 bits of the instruction in IF/ID. However, many instructions (like LUI, AUIPC, JAL, etc.) do not read rs1/rs2, and many immediate instructions do not read rs2. Treating those raw bits as active register source operands causes frequent, unnecessary 1-cycle pipeline stalls, especially for consecutive loads with immediate offsets.\n7:   2. Multiplier combinational overhead: The ALU performs three independent 64-bit multiplications combinationally. This consumes a massive number of LUTs and limits Fmax due to the cascading delay in DSP blocks or carry chains.\n8: hypothesis: |\n9:   We propose two microarchitectural optimizations:\n10:   1. Add precise instruction decode information for rs1/rs2 active read signals (if_id_rs1_read and if_id_rs2_read) into the hazard unit. Only trigger load-use stalls if the dependent instruction actually reads the register.\n11:   2. Replace the three 64-bit multipliers in the ALU with a single shared 33-bit signed multiplier. By dynamically sign-extending or zero-extending the inputs based on the instruction type (signed, unsigned, or signed-unsigned), a single 33-bit signed multiplier can correctly resolve MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU, significantly improving Fmax and reducing LUT4 utilization.\n12: expected_impact:\n13:   fitness_delta_pct: 12\n14:   confidence: high\n15: changes:\n16:   - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n17:     description: \"Update load_use_hazard detection to only trigger when the next instruction in IF/ID actually reads the respective source register (rs1/rs2).\"\n18:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n19:     description: \"Incorporate instruction opcode decoding to dynamically determine if the IF/ID instruction reads rs1 and/or rs2, and wire these to the hazard unit.\"\n20:   - file: rtl/alu.sv\n21:     description: \"Refactor the multiplication logic to use a single, shared 33-bit signed multiplier instead of three independent 64-bit multipliers.\"\n\n(End of file - total 21 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260520-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Precision load-use hazard detection and single 33-bit multiplier sharing\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current 5-stage pipeline has two major microarchitectural bottlenecks:\n  1. Spurious load-use stalls: The hazard unit stalls the pipeline whenever the rd of a load in EX matches the raw rs1/rs2 bits of the instruction in IF/ID. However, many instructions (like LUI, AUIPC, JAL, etc.) do not read rs1/rs2, and many immediate instructions do not read rs2. Treating those raw bits as active register source operands causes frequent, unnecessary 1-cycle pipeline stalls, especially for consecutive loads with immediate offsets.\n  2. Multiplier combinational overhead: The ALU performs three independent 64-bit multiplications combinationally. This consumes a massive number of LUTs and limits Fmax due to the cascading delay in DSP blocks or carry chains.\nhypothesis: |\n  We propose two microarchitectural optimizations:\n  1. Add precise instruction decode information for rs1/rs2 active read signals (if_id_rs1_read and if_id_rs2_read) into the hazard unit. Only trigger load-use stalls if the dependent instruction actually reads the register.\n  2. Replace the three 64-bit multipliers in the ALU with a single shared 33-bit signed multiplier. By dynamically sign-extending or zero-extending the inputs based on the instruction type (signed, unsigned, or signed-unsigned), a single 33-bit signed multiplier can correctly resolve MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU, significantly improving Fmax and reducing LUT4 utilization.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Update load_use_hazard detection to only trigger when the next instruction in IF/ID actually reads the respective source register (rs1/rs2).\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Incorporate instruction opcode decoding to dynamically determine if the IF/ID instruction reads rs1 and/or rs2, and wire these to the hazard unit.\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260520-001-r1s0.yaml","time":{"start":1779280193541,"end":1779280193546}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"AY89a1/1U8JoOUgecNcTubOWdSDJTZ9uDMBkDN/V1gIvyM8aiYzkYt5zcp500npio6AH3FFUao+iwscS2mpBWvkF6XikeQF/TGSEPixpy7nL/HcJ1KfLyvkhgk6a8XiJlXJJNA4Y4ehOyK7Gm3s2HcBf7kwm1CL1/vgrzK/BHDrr+PRkn659AhYfab+cUXA0VXGbRI28ic9qxAXntOc8dkO3SI5wY76Ihg==","id":"tool_read_Fb4wq1TBQlfMkUlCzEQ0","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e455d2801001X98YOvw4H3V5Nd","sessionID":"ses_1baa43bd5ffeTyBXYXMOzEdqBl","messageID":"msg_e455d1455001cFA1GjiqC45HjT"}}
