*\n\nI'm analyzing how splitting the 64-bit order counter into 16-bit chunks affects the carry chain. It appears the logic creates timing isolation between the chunks, as the carry propagation is broken by register-to-enable paths rather than direct adder outputs.\n\n**Confirming 64-bit Increment Logic**\n\nI've mathematically confirmed that the proposed logic for incrementing the split 64-bit order counter is sound. Each 16-bit chunk increments independently, with subsequent chunks only incrementing if the preceding one has overflowed, precisely mirroring a standard 64-bit addition without direct carry propagation between adders.\n\n**Verifying Mux Logic**\n\nI'm examining the multiplexer controls for each 16-bit chunk of the order counter. The logic appears sound: each subsequent chunk's increment is gated by the condition that the preceding chunk has reached its 0xFFFF limit and the `mem_wb_w.valid` signal is asserted.\n\n**Implementing Split Order Counter**\n\nI'm now implementing the split 64-bit order counter logic, mapping the derived conditional increment rules into Verilog. Each 16-bit chunk will increment based on the overflow of the preceding one, synchronized by `mem_wb_w.valid`.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","id":"tool_grep_Kzw5NYR95usS2hOuQx97","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_e45881c730017MlGR58A13R007","sessionID":"ses_1ba783febffeitpXoK9ZCiw5a4","messageID":"msg_e4587f1a0001qkQF5vP9yL0gxW"}}
